{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUI-KV: Efficient GUI Agents via KV Cache with Spatio-Temporal Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:37:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:30:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T23:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) agents built on vision-language models have emerged as a promising approach to automate human-computer workflows. However, they also face the inefficiency challenge as they process long sequences of high-resolution screenshots and solving long-horizon tasks, making inference slow, costly and memory-bound. While key-value (KV) caching can mitigate this, storing the full cache is prohibitive for image-heavy contexts. Existing cache-compression methods are sub-optimal as they do not account for the spatial and temporal redundancy of GUIs. In this work, we first analyze attention patterns in GUI agent workloads and find that, unlike in natural images, attention sparsity is uniformly high across all transformer layers. This insight motivates a simple uniform budget allocation strategy, which we show empirically outperforms more complex layer-varying schemes. Building on this, we introduce GUI-KV, a plug-and-play KV cache compression method for GUI agents that requires no retraining. GUI-KV combines two novel techniques: (i) spatial saliency guidance, which augments attention scores with the L2 norm of hidden states to better preserve semantically important visual tokens, and (ii) temporal redundancy scoring, which projects previous frames' keys onto the current frame's key subspace to preferentially prune redundant history. Across standard GUI agent benchmarks and models, GUI-KV outperforms competitive KV compression baselines, closely matching full-cache accuracy at modest budgets. Notably, in a 5-screenshot setting on the AgentNetBench benchmark, GUI-KV reduces decoding FLOPs by 38.9% while increasing step accuracy by 4.1% over the full-cache baseline. These results demonstrate that exploiting GUI-specific redundancies enables efficient and reliable agent performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by TMLR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin-Qiang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Xin-Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongliang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable yet Noisy Rewards under Imperfect Verifiers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:56:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:56:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T16:20:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:54:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-24T08:25:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-25T01:25:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-22T12:16:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:45:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-05T06:13:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-10-06T01:58:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) replaces costly human labeling with automated verifiers. To reduce verifier hacking, many RLVR systems binarize rewards to $\\{0,1\\}$, but imperfect verifiers inevitably introduce \\emph{false negatives} (rejecting correct answers) and \\emph{false positives} (accepting incorrect ones). We formalize verifier unreliability as a stochastic reward channel with asymmetric noise rates $ρ_0$ and $ρ_1$ -- the FP rate and the FN rate, respectively. From this abstraction we derive two lightweight corrections: (i) a \\emph{backward} correction that yields an unbiased surrogate reward and thus an unbiased policy-gradient estimator in expectation, and (ii) a \\emph{forward} correction that reweights score-function terms so the expected update aligns with the clean gradient direction and requires only the FN rate. We implement both as lightweight hooks in a group relative policy optimization pipeline, both corrections improve RLVR for math reasoning under synthetic and real verifier noise, with the forward variant being more stable under heavier noise. Finally, an appeals mechanism with a lightweight LLM verifier estimates the FN rate online and further improves performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:11:59Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zach","familyName":"Studdiford","name":"Studdiford, Zach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy T.","familyName":"Rogers","name":"Rogers, Timothy T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushin","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Kushin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering the Computational Ingredients of Human-Like Representations in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:37:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:03:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-31T22:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:18:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The human ability to translate diverse perceptual and linguistic inputs into structured behavior has been thought to rest on learning robust representations of concepts. The rapid advancement of transformer-based large language models (LLMs) has surfaced a diversity of computational ingredients relevant for model building - architectures, fine-tuning methods, and training datasets among others - yet it remains unclear which are most crucial for developing human-like conceptual representations. Further, most current benchmarks are ill-suited to measuring representational alignment, making LLMs' scores on them unreliable for assessing whether they are progressing as cognitive models. We address these limitations by evaluating over 75 models on a triplet similarity task, a method well established in cognitive science for measuring conceptual representations, using concepts from the THINGS database. We find that instruction fine-tuning and larger attention head dimensionality are among the strongest predictors of human alignment, while activation function choice, multimodal pretraining, and parameter size have limited influence on alignment. Correlations between alignment scores and existing benchmark scores reveal that while some benchmarks (e.g., BigBenchHard) better capture representational alignment than others (e.g., MUSR), none fully accounts for the variance in human-model alignment, demonstrating their insufficiency. Taken together, our findings highlight key computational ingredients for advancing LLMs as models of human conceptual representation and address a key gap in LLM evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:51Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Xiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yu-Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen-An","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng-Lun","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Sheng-Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-Chun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Po-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsin-Hsi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hsin-Hsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hearing the Order: Investigating Position Bias in Large Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:00:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T09:42:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:36:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T06:24:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:39:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large audio-language models (LALMs) are often used in tasks that involve reasoning over ordered options. An open question is whether their predictions are influenced by the order of answer choices, which would indicate a form of position bias and undermine their reliability. In this paper, we identify and analyze this problem in LALMs. We demonstrate that no model is immune to this bias through extensive experiments on six LALMs across three widely used benchmarks and their spoken counterparts. Shuffling the order of answer options can cause performance fluctuations of up to 24% and even change model rankings, raising concerns about the reliability of current evaluation practices. We also study permutation-based strategies and show that they can mitigate bias in most cases. Our work represents the first systematic investigation of this issue in LALMs, and we hope it raises awareness and motivates further research in this direction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The first two authors contributed equally. Accepted to Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruqian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yijiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annie","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Annie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Syntax-Guided Diffusion Language Models with User-Integrated Personalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:33:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T13:51:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have made revolutionary progress in generating human-like text, yet their outputs often tend to be generic, exhibiting insufficient structural diversity, which limits personalized expression. Recent advances in diffusion models have opened new opportunities for improving language generation beyond the limitations of autoregressive paradigms. In this work, we propose a syntax-guided diffusion language model that integrates structural supervision and personalized conditioning to enhance text quality, diversity, and controllability. We introduce a cascaded framework that generates syntactic guidance before conditional text generation, and further generalize it to a novel noncascaded architecture for better alignment between structure and content. By incorporating syntactic information in the generating process, the proposed model better captures the lexical and structural characteristics of stylistic sentence construction. To enable fine-grained personalization, we develop a shared representation mechanism that facilitates information integration across users, supporting both faithful stylistic generation and generalizable zero-shot inference. Extensive experiments on multiple tasks demonstrate the superiority of our approach in fluency, diversity, and stylistic fidelity. Further qualitative analyses highlight its interpretability and flexibility in learning personalized patterns."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naru","familyName":"Suzuki","name":"Suzuki, Naru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takehiko","familyName":"Ohkawa","name":"Ohkawa, Takehiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatsuro","familyName":"Banno","name":"Banno, Tatsuro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jihyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryosuke","familyName":"Furuta","name":"Furuta, Ryosuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoichi","familyName":"Sato","name":"Sato, Yoichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Affordance-Guided Diffusion Prior for 3D Hand Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:36:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T04:39:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:24:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-08T13:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:34:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How can we reconstruct 3D hand poses when large portions of the hand are heavily occluded by itself or by objects? Humans often resolve such ambiguities by leveraging contextual knowledge -- such as affordances, where an object's shape and function suggest how the object is typically grasped. Inspired by this observation, we propose a generative prior for hand pose refinement guided by affordance-aware textual descriptions of hand-object interactions (HOI). Our method employs a diffusion-based generative model that learns the distribution of plausible hand poses conditioned on affordance descriptions, which are inferred from a large vision-language model (VLM). This enables the refinement of occluded regions into more accurate and functionally coherent hand poses. Extensive experiments on HOGraspNet, a 3D hand-affordance dataset with severe occlusions, demonstrate that our affordance-guided refinement significantly improves hand pose estimation over both recent regression methods and diffusion-based refinement lacking contextual reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026. Project page: https://narusuzuki.github.io/projects/26-affhandgen/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:12:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Aishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"and Bin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, and Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CodeChemist: Test-Time Scaling for Low-Resource Code Generation via Functional Knowledge Transfer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:33:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-22T13:28:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-23T02:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T06:01:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code Large Language Models (CodeLLMs) have been widely adopted for Natural Language to Programming Language code generation, powering applications with large user bases. Their performance, however, varies sharply across programming languages (PLs) and is particularly suboptimal for low-resource PLs due to data scarcity, limiting their overall usability. In this work, we introduce CodeChemist, a simple yet effective, training-free test-time scaling framework that transfers the model's functional knowledge from high-resource to low-resource PLs via synthesized test cases, without relying on external models. Specifically, CodeChemist first applies multi-temperature hedged sampling to generate a pool of candidate solutions in the low-resource PL and synthesizes a set of test inputs. It then estimates uncertainty: when uncertainty is low, it selects the output via in-language majority voting; otherwise, it constructs cross-lingual I/O test oracles by executing high-resource reference programs and selects the candidate with the highest pass rate. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CodeChemist significantly outperforms existing test-time scaling methods, improving code generation for both low-resource PLs (e.g., Lua) and complex-syntax PLs (e.g., C++, Java) without retraining."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted by ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:18Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:13:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunmei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xunmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiantian","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Tiantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhou","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Lingzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Train Large, Deploy Compact: Structured Compression for Compact Low-Rank Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:10:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T04:19:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:20:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T23:06:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:11:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) has become a widely used paradigm for parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large language models, yet its representational capacity often lags behind full fine-tuning. Within the context of LoRA, a key open question is how to obtain expressive low-rank adapters from over-parameterized spaces. We propose \\textit{PrunedLoRA}, a new framework that leverages structured pruning to obtain highly representative low-rank adapters from an over-parameterized initialization. Unlike prior approaches that impose a fixed low-rank budget, PrunedLoRA dynamically prunes less important components during fine-tuning and prevents their reactivation, enabling flexible and adaptive rank allocation. For structured pruning, by minimizing the pruning error for overall loss, we provide fine-grained pruning and recovery updates in a gradient-based pruning strategy with grounded interpretation. We provide the first theoretical analysis of the robustness of structured pruning and provably show that under the impact of weight perturbation, gradient-based pruning is more robust than activation-based pruning with respect to overall loss. Empirically, PrunedLoRA consistently outperforms LoRA and its variants across supervised fine-tuning tasks in mathematical reasoning, code generation, and natural language understanding, and it also demonstrates advantages over existing structured pruning methods across diverse sparsity levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:43Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanpei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weifeng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Weifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVLA: On Robustness of Vision-Language-Action Model against Multi-Modal Perturbations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T14:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T09:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T07:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:43:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-27T06:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Vision-Language-Actionf(VLA) models, robustness to real-world perturbations is critical for deployment. Existing methods target simple visual disturbances, overlooking the broader multi-modal perturbations that arise in actions, instructions, environments, and observations. Here, we first evaluate the robustness of mainstream VLAs under 17 perturbations across four modalities. We find (1) actions as the most fragile modality, (2) Existing visual-robust VLA do not gain robustness in other modality, and (3) pi0 demonstrates superior robustness. To build multi-modal robust VLAs, we propose RobustVLA against perturbations in VLA inputs and outputs. For output robustness, we perform offline robust optimization against worst-case action noise that maximizes mismatch in flow matching objective. This can be seen as adversarial training, label smoothing, and outlier penalization. For input robustness, we enforce consistent actions across input variations that preserve task semantics. To account for multiple perturbations, we formulate robustness as a multi-armed bandit problem and apply an upper confidence bound algorithm to automatically identify the most harmful noise. Experiments on LIBERO demonstrate our RobustVLA delivers absolute gains over baselines of 12.6% on the pi0 backbone and 10.4% on the OpenVLA backbone across all 17 perturbations, achieving 50.6x faster inference than existing visual-robust BYOVLA that requires external LLMs, and a 10.4% gain under mixed perturbations. On the real-world FR5 robot, under four types of multimodal perturbations, RobustVLA shows strong low-data performance, outperforming pi0 by 65.6% success rate with 25 demonstrations. Even with abundant demos, our method still outperform pi0 by 30% success rate. Code and demo videos available at https://github.com/gakakulicc/RobustVLA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge","familyName":"Mendez-Mendez","name":"Mendez-Mendez, Jorge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Systematic Study of Large Language Models for Task and Motion Planning With PDDLStream"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:03:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-17T15:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-20T00:48:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While we know that large language models (LLMs) can solve some planning problems, we do not understand the extent of these capabilities for robotics. One promising direction is to integrate the semantic knowledge of LLMs with the formal reasoning of task and motion planning (TAMP). However, designing such systems is complicated by the myriad of choices for how to integrate LLMs within TAMP. We develop 16 algorithms that use LLMs to substitute key TAMP components. Our zero-shot experiments across 13750 evaluations and three domains reveal that LLM-based planners exhibit lower success rates and higher planning times than engineered systems. Providing geometric details increases the number of task-planning errors compared to pure PDDL descriptions, and (faster) direct LLM variants outperform (slower) reasoning variants in most cases. Code and results are available at https://github.com/jorge-a-mendez/llm-pddlstream."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2026 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems (IROS). 8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:28Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-20T02:22:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Sicilia","name":"Sicilia, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael R.","familyName":"Tomz","name":"Tomz, Michael R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher D.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Christopher D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verbalized Sampling: How to Mitigate Mode Collapse and Unlock LLM Diversity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:55:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T16:29:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T17:38:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-13T01:03:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-15T22:07:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-17T00:12:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training alignment often reduces LLM diversity, leading to a phenomenon known as mode collapse. Unlike prior work that attributes this effect to algorithmic limitations, we identify a fundamental, pervasive data-level driver: typicality bias in preference data, whereby annotators systematically favor familiar text as a result of well-established findings in cognitive psychology. We formalize this bias theoretically, verify it on preference datasets empirically, and show that it plays a central role in mode collapse. Motivated by this analysis, we introduce Verbalized Sampling, a simple, training-free prompting strategy to circumvent mode collapse. VS prompts the model to verbalize a probability distribution over a set of responses (e.g., \"Generate 5 jokes about coffee and their corresponding probabilities\"). Comprehensive experiments show that VS significantly improves performance across creative writing (poems, stories, jokes), dialogue simulation, open-ended QA, and synthetic data generation, without sacrificing factual accuracy and safety. For instance, in creative writing, VS increases diversity by 1.6-2.1x over direct prompting. We further observe an emergent trend that more capable models benefit more from VS. In sum, our work provides a new data-centric perspective on mode collapse and a practical inference-time remedy that helps unlock pre-trained generative diversity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"83 pages, 31 figures, 44 tables. Code is available at https://github.com/CHATS-lab/verbalize-sampling"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:22Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-17T02:33:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Bok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dong Bok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seanie","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangwoo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sangwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minki","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Minki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinheon","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Jinheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiongdao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiongdao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heejun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Heejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Bocklet","name":"Bocklet, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Ju","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Sung Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiang","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Reward Models for Multi-Domain Test-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:21:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T02:37:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:20:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-13T23:08:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-15T00:14:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reliability of large language models (LLMs) during test-time scaling is often assessed with \\emph{external verifiers} or \\emph{reward models} that distinguish correct reasoning from flawed logic. Prior work has studied both outcome reward models (ORMs), which assess only the final answer, and process reward models (PRMs), which score intermediate reasoning steps. Although PRMs are often viewed as advantageous due to their finer-grained supervision, much of the supporting evidence comes from math-adjacent settings, and their relative benefits across broader domains remain unclear. We present the first unified evaluation of four reward model variants, discriminative ORM and PRM (dORM, dPRM) and generative ORM and PRM (gORM, gPRM), across 14 diverse domains. Contrary to conventional wisdom, we find that (i) dORM performs on par with dPRM, (ii) gPRM is not competitive, and (iii) overall, gORM is the most robust, yielding significant and consistent gains across every tested domain. We attribute the worse performance of gPRM to the stepwise scoring process, which inherits label noise from LLM-based automatic labeling, leading to difficulties in evaluating long reasoning trajectories, including those involving self-correcting reasoning. Both our theoretical analysis and empirical observations indicate that stepwise aggregation compounds errors as reasoning length increases. These findings challenge the common assumption that fine-grained supervision is always better and support generative outcome verification for multi-domain deployment. Our \\href{https://github.com/db-Lee/Multi-RM}{\\underline{code}} is publicly available to facilitate future research in multi-domain settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:04Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:16:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songtao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Songtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pin-Yu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Pin-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Can Mamba Learn In Context with Outliers and Generalize Provably?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:18:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-07T00:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-08T00:14:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Mamba model has gained significant attention for its computational advantages over Transformer-based models, while achieving comparable performance across a wide range of language tasks. Like Transformers, Mamba exhibits in-context learning (ICL) capabilities, i.e., making predictions for new tasks based on a prompt containing input-label pairs and a query, without requiring fine-tuning. Despite its empirical success, the theoretical understanding of Mamba remains limited, largely due to the nonlinearity introduced by its gating mechanism. To the best of our knowledge, this paper presents the first theoretical analysis of the training dynamics of a one-layer Mamba model, which consists of a linear attention component followed by a nonlinear gating layer, and its ICL generalization on unseen binary classification tasks, even when the prompt includes additive outliers. Our analysis shows that Mamba leverages the linear attention layer to select informative context examples and uses the nonlinear gating layer to suppress the influence of outliers. By establishing and comparing to the analysis of linear Transformers under the same setting, we show that although Mamba may require more training iterations to converge, it maintains accurate predictions even when the proportion of outliers exceeds the threshold that a linear Transformer can tolerate. These theoretical findings are supported by empirical experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:45Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-08T02:09:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sanghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Alaniz","name":"Alaniz, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqin","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Yongqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeynep","familyName":"Akata","name":"Akata, Zeynep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training-free Uncertainty Guidance for Complex Visual Tasks with MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:42:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-13T13:45:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T00:49:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T15:22:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-29T00:53:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) often struggle with fine-grained perception, such as identifying small objects in high-resolution images or detecting key moments in long videos. Existing methods typically rely on complex, task-specific fine-tuning, which reduces generalizability and increases system complexity. In this work, we propose an effective, training-free framework that uses an MLLM's intrinsic uncertainty as proactive guidance. Our core insight is that a model's uncertainty decreases when provided with relevant visual information. We introduce a unified mechanism that scores candidate visual inputs by response uncertainty, enabling the model to autonomously focus on the most informative data. We apply this simple principle to three challenging visual tasks: Visual Search, Long Video Understanding, and Temporal Grounding, allowing off-the-shelf MLLMs to achieve performance competitive with specialized, fine-tuned systems. Our results demonstrate that leveraging intrinsic uncertainty is a powerful strategy for improving fine-grained multimodal performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:47Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-29T01:59:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waïss","familyName":"Azizian","name":"Azizian, Waïss","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Does the Pretraining Distribution Shape In-Context Learning? A Fundamental Trade-Off"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.3; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:52:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:10:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T11:54:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:50:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The factors driving the performance of in-context learning (ICL) in large language models (LLMs) remain poorly understood despite ICL's surprising effectiveness, enabling models to adapt to new tasks from only a handful of examples. To clarify and improve these capabilities, we characterize how the statistical properties of the pretraining distribution (e.g., tail behavior, coverage) shape ICL. We develop a theoretical framework that encompasses generalization and task selection and show how distributional properties govern sample efficiency, task retrieval, and robustness. To this end, we generalize existing concentration results to heavy-tailed priors and dependent sequences, better reflecting the structure of LLM pretraining data. Our framework reveals a fundamental design trade-off: heavy-tailed pretraining distributions facilitate robust task selection under distribution shifts but are detrimental to generalization, especially in low-data regimes. We then empirically evaluate our predictions by studying how ICL performance varies with the pretraining distribution on challenging tasks such as stochastic differential equations and stochastic processes with memory. Together, these findings suggest that controlling key statistical properties of the pretraining distribution is essential for building ICL-capable and reliable LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"57 pages, 15 figures; to be presented at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dipankar","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Dipankar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuyuki","familyName":"Tokuda","name":"Tokuda, Fuyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norman C.","familyName":"Tien","name":"Tien, Norman C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuhiro","familyName":"Kosuge","name":"Kosuge, Kazuhiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RTFF: Random-to-Target Fabric Flattening Policy using Dual-Arm Manipulator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:20:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:50:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T03:39:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:23:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic fabric manipulation remains challenging due to fabric deformability and occlusions from wrinkles and the manipulator. This paper defines Random-to-Target Fabric Flattening (RTFF) as the task of bringing a randomly wrinkled fabric to an arbitrary user-specified wrinkle-free target pose. RTFF requires simultaneous flattening and pose alignment, where the two objectives are inherently coupled since flattening the fabric displaces its pose, while realigning it tends to introduce wrinkles. To solve this task, this paper anchors both the current and target fabric states to the same template mesh, enabling direct vertex-level wrinkle and pose assessment without registration. Building on this representation, a hybrid Imitation Learning--Visual Servoing (IL--VS) RTFF policy is proposed. A novel Mesh Action Chunking Transformer (MACT) leverages structured mesh observations to achieve goal-conditioned coarse alignment from a compact demonstration set, after which VS ensures precise convergence to the target. The policy is validated on a real dual-arm teleoperation system, demonstrating precise alignment to unseen target poses, fabric types, and scales. Code and videos: https://kaitang98.github.io/RTFF_Policy/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures, conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:33Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandrine","familyName":"Fortier","name":"Fortier, Alexandrine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Thebaud","name":"Thebaud, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesús","familyName":"Villalba","name":"Villalba, Jesús","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najim","familyName":"Dehak","name":"Dehak, Najim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Cardinal","name":"Cardinal, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"West","name":"West, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Do Backdoors Live? A Component-Level Analysis of Backdoor Propagation in Speech Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:45:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:10:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-13T03:59:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-14T01:19:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T17:07:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-08T01:10:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T20:25:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech language models (SLMs) are systems of systems: independent components that unite to achieve a common goal. Despite their heterogeneous nature, SLMs are often studied end-to-end; how information flows through the pipeline remains obscure. We investigate this question through the lens of backdoor attacks. We first establish that backdoors can propagate through the SLM, leaving all tasks highly vulnerable. From this, we design a component analysis to discover the role each component takes in backdoor learning. We find that backdoor persistence or erasure is highly dependent on the targeted component. Beyond propagation, we examine how backdoors are encoded in shared multitask embeddings, showing that poisoned samples are not directly separable from benign ones, challenging a common separability assumption used in filtering defenses. Our findings emphasize the need to treat multimodal pipelines as intricate systems with unique vulnerabilities, not solely extensions of unimodal ones."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Interspeech 2026 (long paper)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:01Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-11T02:25:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00054","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00054","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00054","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiman","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yiman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiong","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiDe: Rethinking The Zoom-IN method in High Resolution MLLMs via Hierarchical Decoupling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T08:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T17:51:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T01:20:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-04T08:28:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:42:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have made significant strides in visual understanding tasks. However, their performance on high-resolution images remains suboptimal. While existing approaches often attribute this limitation to perceptual constraints and argue that MLLMs struggle to recognize small objects, leading them to use \"zoom in\" strategies for better detail, our analysis reveals a different cause: the main issue is not object size, but rather caused by complex background interference. We systematically analyze this \"zoom in\" operation through a series of decoupling experiments and propose the Hierarchical Decoupling Framework (HiDe), a training-free framework that uses Token-wise Attention Decoupling (TAD) to decouple the question tokens and identify the key information tokens, then leverages their attention weights to achieve precise alignment with the target visual regions. Subsequently, it employs Layout-Preserving Decoupling (LPD) to decouple these regions from the background and reconstructs a compact representation that preserves essential spatial layouts while eliminating background interference. HiDe sets a new SOTA on V*Bench, HRBench4K, and HRBench8K, boosting Qwen2.5-VL 7B and InternVL3 8B to SOTA (92.1% and 91.6% on V*Bench), even surpassing RL methods. After optimization, HiDe uses 75% less memory than the previous training-free approach. Code is provided in https://tennine2077.github.io/HiDe.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICML2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00054","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:19Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasad Rajesh","familyName":"Posture","name":"Posture, Prasad Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashrajsinh","familyName":"Mahida","name":"Mahida, Yashrajsinh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Noble","name":"Noble, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CosmoUiT: A Vision Transformer-UNet Hybrid for Fast and Accurate Emulation of 21-cm Maps from the Epoch of Reionization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:07:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-03T17:40:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-04T01:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1475-7516/2026/06/011","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The observation of the redshifted 21-cm signal from the intergalactic medium will probe the epoch of reionization (EoR) with unprecedented detail. Various simulations are being developed and used to predict and understand the nature and morphology of this signal. However, these simulations are computationally very expensive and time-consuming to produce in large numbers. To overcome this problem, an efficient field-level emulator of this signal is required. However, the EoR 21-cm signal is highly non-Gaussian; therefore, capturing the correlations between different scales of this signal, which is directly related to the evolution of the reionization, with the neural network is quite difficult. Here, we introduce CosmoUiT, a UNet integrated vision transformer-based architecture, to overcome these difficulties. CosmoUiT emulates the 3D cubes of 21-cm signal from the EoR, for a given input dark matter density field, halo density field, and reionization parameters. CosmoUiT uses the multi-head self-attention mechanism of the transformer to capture the long-range dependencies and convolutional layers in the UNet to capture the small-scale variations in the target 21-cm field. Furthermore, the training of the emulator is conditioned on the input reionization parameters such that it gives a fast and accurate prediction of the 21-cm field for different sets of input reionization parameters. We evaluate the predictions of our emulator by comparing various statistics (e.g., bubble size distribution, power spectrum) and morphological features of the emulated and simulated maps. We further demonstrate that this vision transformer-based architecture can emulate the entire 3D 21-cm signal cube with high accuracy at both large and small scales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published version; 38 pages, 23 figures, and 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:28:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prithviraj","familyName":"Ammanabrolu","name":"Ammanabrolu, Prithviraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simultaneous Multi-objective Alignment Across Verifiable and Non-verifiable Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:54:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-30T06:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning large language models to human preferences is inherently multidimensional, yet most pipelines collapse heterogeneous signals into a single objective. We seek to answer what it would take to simultaneously align a model across various domains spanning those with: verifiable rewards, non-verifiable subjective preferences, and complex interactive scenarios. Such multi-objective alignment setups are often plagued by individual objectives being at odds with each other, resulting in inefficient training and limited user control during inference. To address these issues, we propose $\\textbf{M}$ulti-$\\textbf{A}$ction-$\\textbf{H}$ead $\\textbf{AL}$ignment with PRM-guided Dec$\\textbf{O}$ding ($\\textbf{MAHALO}$), a unified framework that standardizes PRM training across verifiable and non-verifiable settings for step-level supervision, performs vectorized multi-objective alignment with Multi-Action-Head DPO, and enables controllable inference through objective-specific weighting and PRM-guided decoding. Experiments across math reasoning, human values alignment, and multi-turn tutoring show that MAHALO jointly improves multiple objectives simultaneously with limited interference, while remaining generalizable and adaptable across domains and offering flexible user control at inference time. Our code is available at: https://github.com/pearls-lab/multiobj-align."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Mingjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingjun","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Xiaohe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaohe","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Beihang University"],"givenName":"Menghao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Menghao","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Ziteng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziteng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Yanmin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yanmin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Da","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Da","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Beihang University"],"givenName":"Fangzheng","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Fangzheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jun","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"He","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, He","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Tsinghua University"],"givenName":"Aohan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Aohan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zhipu AI"],"givenName":"Shuaixing","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Shuaixing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Ruya","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Ruya","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Yang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["China Unicom Research Institute"],"givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bowen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Infrawaves"],"givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wenqi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Shanghai Innovation Institute"],"givenName":"Jinlong","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Jinlong","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Shanghai Innovation Institute"],"givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yuan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Shanghai AI Power Technology Co., Ltd"],"givenName":"Hongzhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongzhou","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["China Unicom Research Institute"],"givenName":"Bohua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Bohua","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Tsinghua University"],"givenName":"Mingwei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingwei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Beihang University"],"givenName":"Chunming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chunming","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Efficient, Reliable and Observable Collective Communication Library in Large-scale GPU Training Clusters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:00:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T03:09:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale LLM training requires collective communication libraries to exchange data among distributed GPUs. As a company dedicated to building and operating large-scale GPU training clusters, we encounter several practical limitations of NCCL in production, including 1) SM competition between computation and communication, 2) expensive restart costs under link failures, and 3) insufficient observability of transient collective communication anomalies. To address these challenges, we propose VCCL, an efficient, reliable, and observable collective communication library in large-scale GPU training clusters. VCCL removes SM-consuming P2P kernels by moving intra-node data movement and stream dependency enforcement to CPU threads and GPU copy engines. VCCL also introduces a primary-backup QP mechanism to tolerate frequent NIC port failures, and designs a window-based monitor to observe network anomalies at O(μs) level. We opensource VCCL and deploy it in production training clusters for several months. Compared with NCCL, VCCL improves training throughput by up to 5.28% and reduces massive GPU resource wastage through runtime fault tolerance and finegrained monitor. We also share experience and lessons we learned during the deployment of VCCL in large-scale clusters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 21 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01009","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01009","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01009","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashim","familyName":"Dahal","name":"Dahal, Ashim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Ghimire","name":"Ghimire, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saydul Akbar","familyName":"Murad","name":"Murad, Saydul Akbar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Rahimi","name":"Rahimi, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"POVQA: Preference-Optimized Video Question Answering with Rationales for Data Efficiency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:15:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:01:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-24T16:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-25T01:14:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T19:53:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:22:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-video multimodal question answering requires structured reasoning over visual evidence and dialogue, but Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) are constrained by context-window and compute limits. We propose POVQA, which compresses each second into a temporally pooled image (1 fps pooled images) to maintain dense temporal coverage under a fixed token budget. We then train Qwen2.5-VL-7B with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on rationale+answer targets, and optionally apply Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) for preference alignment. We introduce ReasonVQA as a pilot diagnostic dataset with 12 movies and 239 human-annotated QA+rationale triplets for controlled analysis of long-context multimodal reasoning under compression. On ReasonVQA, SFT improves the best pooled-only baseline from 0.212 to 0.550 F1, showing that pooled evidence plus rationale supervision provides the main performance gains in this setting. In zero-shot transfer, POVQA also reaches 64.7\\% on TVQA after SFT+DPO. These results are preliminary: ReasonVQA is small, pooling can lose fine-grained temporal order, and DPO effects are not uniformly positive across settings. Code, dataset, and additional qualitative evaluations are available at \\href{https://povqa.github.io}{https://povqa.github.io}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in MAR at CVPR Workshop (Proceedings Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01009","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minki","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Minki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Ning","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei-Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongge","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Dongge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huseyin A.","familyName":"Inan","name":"Inan, Huseyin A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Wutschitz","name":"Wutschitz, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Sim","name":"Sim, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saravan","familyName":"Rajmohan","name":"Rajmohan, Saravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACON: Optimizing Context Compression for Long-horizon LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T06:48:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T07:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:45:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as agents in dynamic real-world environments, where success depends on maintaining precise records of actions and observations. However, the resulting unbounded context growth in long-horizon agentic tasks makes two critical bottlenecks: prohibitive inference memory costs and reasoning degradation due to irrelevant information. Existing compression methods fail to fully address this, often relying on brittle heuristics or requiring parameter updates impractical for proprietary or large-scale LLMs. We introduce Agent Context Optimization (ACON), a unified framework that optimally compresses both observations and history into concise, informative representations. Distinct from prior works, ACON employs an optimization in natural language space: it iteratively refines compression guidelines based on failure analysis of the agent, ensuring critical state information is preserved without model fine-tuning. To further minimize computational overhead, we distill the optimized compressor into smaller models. Experiments on AppWorld, OfficeBench, and Multi-objective QA demonstrate that ACON reduces peak token usage by 26-54% while improving task success over existing compression baselines. Notably, it enables smaller LMs to function effectively as long-horizon agents, achieving up to 46% performance improvement by mitigating context distraction. Our code is available at https://github.com/microsoft/acon."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:12Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00481","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00481","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00481","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antariksh","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Antariksh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zili","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make a Video Call with LLM: A Measurement Campaign over Six Mainstream Apps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:03:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-30T15:21:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:43:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In 2025, Large Language Model (LLM) services have launched a new feature -- AI video chat -- allowing users to interact with AI agents via real-time video communication (RTC), just like chatting with real people. Despite its significance, no systematic study has characterized the performance of existing AI video chat systems. To address this gap, this paper proposes a comprehensive benchmark across four dimensions: quality, latency, internal mechanisms, and system overhead. Using custom testbeds, we further evaluate six mainstream AI video chatbots with this benchmark. We also build an online platform for user study. The measurement leads to interesting findings that could be beneficial to the future optimizations. For example, the network latency of AI video chat matters not as much as human video chat. The capabilities of AI agents matters most in the user experience. Our benchmarking results also open up several research questions for future optimizations of AI video chatbots. Availability: https://callarena.net/ for the online evaluation platform and our open-sourced dataset and testbed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00481","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:47Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Dadsetan","name":"Dadsetan, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Rudzicz","name":"Rudzicz, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Re-examining Low Rank adaptation for private LLM fine-tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:25:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:09:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T15:29:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-12T01:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T18:48:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-01T00:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Privacy is a central concern when fine-tuning large language models (LLMs) on sensitive data, and differentially private stochastic gradient descent (DP-SGD) -- which clips per-sample gradients and adds calibrated Gaussian noise -- is the standard tool for formal privacy guarantees. Both theory and practice show that lower-rank models are better suited to DP training, a property especially relevant for LLMs, whose fine-tuning gradients exhibit a strong low-rank structure. Methods such as DP-LoRA exploit this by restricting updates to a low-rank subspace, i.e., retaining only a few non-zero components in the SVD of each layer's gradient. However, we argue that while having few non-zero components is important, the isotropic noise injected by DP-SGD inflates the singular values of the gradient matrix, disrupting their naturally fast decay. In this work, we investigate whether this noise-induced eigenvalue blow-up reduces performance, and show that partially restoring the original singular-value profile significantly improves the sample efficiency of DP-SGD. Experiments on language classification (GLUE benchmark with RoBERTa) and text generation (E2E and DART table-to-text benchmarks with Qwen and Llama models up to 4B parameters) showcase that restoring the fast decay of singular values is a viable strategy for speeding up the DP optimization process, without compromising privacy guarantees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:32Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00419","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00419","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00419","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kairun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kairun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning a Zeroth-Order Optimizer for Fine-Tuning LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:01:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-29T06:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T00:35:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zeroth-order optimizers have recently emerged as an attractive approach for fine-tuning large language models (LLMs), as they avoid backpropagation and can substantially reduce memory overhead relative to standard first-order training. However, existing zeroth-order methods rely on hand-crafted, static sampling strategies that are not adaptable to model-specific structures. To address this, we propose ZO-Finetuner, a learning-based zeroth-order optimizer for LLMs that automatically learns efficient perturbation strategies through a compact and memory-efficient design. Motivated by the fact that a small set of base LLMs is repeatedly fine-tuned across tasks, ZO-Finetuner supports one-time per-model training and reuse across downstream tasks with minimal overhead. Therefore, learning the optimizer once for a given LLM and reusing it across diverse downstream tasks is both feasible and highly desirable. Accordingly, ZO-Finetuner is designed to scale learning to learn (L2L) to the foundation-model era by supporting one-time per-model training with minimal overhead. Experiments on 4 LLMs and 7 datasets show that ZO-Finetuner outperforms prior zeroth-order baselines in 82.1\\% of task-model combinations, thereby demonstrating strong performance and scalability for efficient LLM fine-tuning. The code can be found in https://github.com/ASTRAL-Group/ZO_Fine_tuner."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00419","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:14Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00777","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00777","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00777","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngbin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Youngbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Minjong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saemi","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Saemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyuk","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Seunghyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaehyeon","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Chaehyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MoonJeong","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, MoonJeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-Place Feedback: Reliable Refinement for Multi-Turn Expert-LLM Collaboration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T11:16:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:47:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T05:41:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:33:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-generated drafts often contain subtle factual or logical errors, yet prior work shows that models struggle to reliably integrate multi-turn feedback aimed at fixing them. We propose in-place feedback, an interaction paradigm in which the user directly edits the model's previous response and the model continues generation from the edited context. In-place feedback consistently outperforms standard multi-turn feedback across five reasoning-intensive benchmarks while requiring fewer tokens, and our fine-grained analysis shows that it applies corrections more reliably and propagates them to subsequent reasoning. A user study with domain experts refining LLM-generated summaries corroborates these findings: participants report higher final-output satisfaction and substantially lower fatigue with in-place feedback, and a mixed strategy combining in-place and multi-turn feedback scores highest on every measured dimension. These results suggest that editing errors directly is a more effective paradigm for expert-LLM collaboration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00777","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:37Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:56:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00526","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00526","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00526","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaotang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Gaotang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhong","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Ruizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiusi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiusi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanghang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Hanghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Log Likelihood: Probability-Based Objectives for Supervised Fine-Tuning across the Model Capability Continuum"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:17:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:29:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T05:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:27:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T01:00:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:14:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) is the standard approach for post-training large language models (LLMs), yet it often shows limited generalization. We trace this limitation to its default training objective: negative log likelihood (NLL). While NLL is classically optimal when training from scratch, post-training operates in a different paradigm and could violate its optimality assumptions, where models already encode task-relevant priors and supervision can be long and noisy. In this work, we systematically study various probability-based objectives and characterize when and why different objectives succeed or fail under varying conditions. Through comprehensive experiments and extensive ablation studies across 8 model backbones, 27 benchmarks, and 7 domains, we uncover a critical dimension that governs objective behavior: the model-capability continuum. Near the model-strong end, prior-leaning objectives that downweight low-probability tokens (e.g., $-p$, $-p^{10}$, thresholded variants) consistently outperform NLL; toward the model-weak end, NLL dominates; in between, no single objective prevails. Our theoretical analysis further elucidates how objectives trade places across the continuum, providing a principled foundation for adapting objectives to model capability. The code is available at https://github.com/GaotangLi/Beyond-Log-Likelihood."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00526","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:01Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-25T02:09:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00319","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00319","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00319","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DecepChain: Inducing Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T15:10:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T01:03:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have been demonstrating strong reasoning capability with their chain-of-thoughts (CoT), which are routinely used by humans to judge answer quality. This reliance creates a powerful yet fragile basis for trust. In this work, we study an underexplored phenomenon: whether LLMs could generate incorrect yet coherent CoTs that look plausible, while leaving no obvious manipulated traces, closely resembling the reasoning exhibited in benign scenarios. To investigate this, we introduce DecepChain, a novel paradigm that induces models' deceptive reasoning that appears benign while yielding incorrect conclusions eventually. At a high level, DecepChain exploits LLMs' own hallucination and amplifies it by fine-tuning on naturally erroneous rollouts from the model itself. Then, it reinforces it via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a flipped reward on triggered inputs, plus a rule-based format reward to preserve fluent, benign-looking reasoning. Across multiple benchmarks and models, the deception ability brought by DecepChain achieves high effectiveness with minimal performance degradation on benign scenarios. Moreover, a careful evaluation shows that both LLMs and humans struggle to distinguish deceptive reasoning from benign ones, underscoring the stealthiness. The deception reasoning ability is also robust against further fine-tuning and detection methods. Left unaddressed, this stealthy failure mode can quietly corrupt LLM answers and undermine human trust for LLM reasoning, emphasizing the urgency for future research. Project page: https://decepchain.github.io/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00319","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-22T02:29:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Mazzaglia","name":"Mazzaglia, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cansu","familyName":"Sancaktar","name":"Sancaktar, Cansu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Peschl","name":"Peschl, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dijkman","name":"Dijkman, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Training for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:27:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T06:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using Large Language Models to produce intermediate thoughts, a.k.a. Chain-of-thought (CoT), before providing an answer has been a successful recipe for solving complex language tasks. In robotics, similar embodied CoT strategies, generating thoughts before actions, have also been shown to lead to improved performance when using Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs). As these techniques increase the length of the model's generated outputs to include the thoughts, the inference time is negatively affected. Delaying an agent's actions in real-world executions, as in robotic manipulation settings, strongly affects the usability of a method, as tasks require long sequences of actions. However, is the generation of long chains-of-thought a strong prerequisite for achieving performance improvements? In this work, we explore the idea of Hybrid Training (HyT), a framework that enables VLAs to learn from thoughts and benefit from the associated performance gains, while enabling the possibility to leave out CoT generation during inference. Furthermore, by learning to conditionally predict a diverse set of outputs, HyT supports flexibility at inference time, enabling the model to either predict actions directly, generate thoughts or follow instructions. We evaluate the proposed method in a series of simulated benchmarks and real-world experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia-Chen","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jia-Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Energy-Regularized Sequential Model Editing on Hyperspheres"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:55:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T09:55:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:54:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T05:50:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:32:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) require constant updates to remain aligned with evolving real-world knowledge. Model editing offers a lightweight alternative to retraining, but sequential editing often destabilizes representations and induces catastrophic forgetting. In this work, we seek to better understand and mitigate performance degradation caused by sequential editing. We hypothesize that hyperspherical uniformity, a property that maintains uniform distribution of neuron weights on a hypersphere, helps the model remain stable, retain prior knowledge, while still accommodate new updates. We use Hyperspherical Energy (HE) to quantify neuron uniformity during editing, and examine its correlation with editing performance. Empirical studies across widely used editing methods reveals a strong correlation between HE dynamics and editing performance, with editing failures consistently coinciding with high HE fluctuations. We further theoretically prove that HE dynamics impose a lower bound on the degradation of pretrained knowledge, highlighting why HE stability is crucial for knowledge retention. Motivated by these insights, we propose SPHERE (Sparse Projection for Hyperspherical Energy-Regularized Editing), an HE-driven regularization strategy that stabilizes neuron weight distributions, ultimately preserving prior knowledge while enabling reliable sequential updates. Specifically, SPHERE identifies a sparse space complementary to the principal hyperspherical directions of the pretrained weight matrices and projects new knowledge onto it, attenuating perturbations on the principal directions. Extensive experiments on LLaMA3 (8B) and Qwen2.5 (7B) show that SPHERE outperforms the best baseline in editing capability by an average of 16.41%, while most faithfully preserving general model performance, thereby offering a principled path toward reliable large-scale knowledge editing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026. The code is available at https://github.com/PlusLabNLP/SPHERE. Project page: https://www.qingyuanliu.net/sphere_projectpage/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:23Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liheng","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Liheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Muzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingxue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Coates","name":"Coates, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian-Yun","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jian-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"It Takes Two: Your GRPO Is Secretly DPO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:15:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-14T04:25:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:28:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GRPO has emerged as a prominent reinforcement learning algorithm for post-training LLMs. Unlike critic-based methods, GRPO computes advantages by estimating the \\emph{value baselines} from group-level statistics, eliminating the need for a critic network. Consequently, the prevailing view emphasizes the necessity of large group sizes, which are assumed to yield more accurate statistical estimates. In this paper, we propose a different view that the efficacy of GRPO stems from its implicit contrastive objective in the optimization, which helps reduce variance via the control variate method. This makes GRPO structurally related to preference learning methods such as DPO. This perspective motivates 2-GRPO, a minimal group-size variant that constructs contrastive signals with only two rollouts. We provide a rigorous theoretical analysis of 2-GRPO and empirically validate its effectiveness: 2-GRPO retains $97.6\\%$ of the performance of 16-GRPO, while requiring only $12.5\\%$ of the rollouts and $21\\%$ of the training time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00231","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00231","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00231","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Geh","name":"Geh, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guy Van den","familyName":"Broeck","name":"Broeck, Guy Van den","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Israel","name":"Israel, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Pitfalls of KV Cache Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:55:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T19:46:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:08:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV cache compression promises increased throughput and efficiency with negligible loss in performance. While the gains in throughput are indisputable and recent literature has indeed shown minimal degradation on particular benchmarks, in general the consequences of compression in realistic scenarios such as multi-instruction prompting have been insufficiently studied. In this paper, we identify several pitfalls that practitioners should be aware of when deploying KV cache compressed LLMs. We evaluate five KV cache compression methods (StreamingLLM, SnapKV, TOVA, H2O, and K-Norm) on Llama3.1 8B and Qwen2.5 14B under multi-instruction prompting with IFEval. Importantly, we show that certain instructions degrade much more rapidly with compression, effectively causing them to be completely ignored by the LLM. As a practical example, we highlight system prompt leakage as a case study, empirically demonstrating the impact of compression on leakage and general instruction-following. We identify several factors that contribute to system prompt leakage: compression method, instruction order, and KV eviction bias. We then propose simple changes to KV cache eviction policies that can reduce the impact of these factors and improve the overall performance in multi-instruction tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00231","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:42Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00883","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00883","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00883","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose I.","familyName":"Mestre","name":"Mestre, Jose I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Fernández-Hernández","name":"Fernández-Hernández, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristian","familyName":"Pérez-Corral","name":"Pérez-Corral, Cristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel F.","familyName":"Dolz","name":"Dolz, Manuel F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Duato","name":"Duato, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique S.","familyName":"Quintana-Ortí","name":"Quintana-Ortí, Enrique S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAI: GreenLightningAI for Accelerated Training through Knowledge Decoupling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:31:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:54:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T08:34:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:53:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work we introduce GreenLightningAI (GLAI), a new architectural block designed as an alternative to conventional MLPs. The central idea is to separate two types of knowledge that are usually entangled during training: (i) *structural knowledge*, encoded by the stable activation patterns induced by ReLU activations; and (ii) *quantitative knowledge*, carried by the numerical weights and biases. By fixing the structure once stabilized, GLAI reformulates the MLP as a combination of paths, where only the quantitative component is optimized. This reformulation retains the universal approximation capabilities of MLPs, yet achieves a more efficient training process, reducing training time by ~40% on average across the cases examined in this study. Crucially, GLAI is not just another classifier, but a generic block that can replace MLPs wherever they are used, from supervised heads with frozen backbones to projection layers in self-supervised learning or few-shot classifiers. Across diverse experimental setups, GLAI consistently matches or exceeds the accuracy of MLPs with an equivalent number of parameters, while converging faster. Overall, GLAI establishes a new design principle that opens a direction for future integration into large-scale architectures such as Transformers, where MLP blocks dominate the computational footprint."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00883","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:11:14Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:11:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:03:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReSeek: A Self-Correcting Framework for Search Agents with Instructive Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:33:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T08:22:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:35:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T11:30:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:54:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Search agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated significant potential in tackling knowledge-intensive tasks. Reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for training these agents to perform complex, multi-step reasoning. However, prior RL-based methods often rely on sparse or rule-based rewards, which can lead agents to commit to suboptimal or erroneous reasoning paths without the ability to recover. To address these limitations, we propose ReSeek, a novel self-correcting framework for training search agents. Our framework introduces a self-correction mechanism that empowers the agent to dynamically identify and recover from erroneous search paths during an episode. By invoking a special JUDGE action, the agent can judge the information and re-plan its search strategy. To guide this process, we design a dense, instructive process reward function, which decomposes into a correctness reward for retrieving factual information and a utility reward for finding information genuinely useful for the query. Furthermore, to mitigate the risk of data contamination in existing datasets, we introduce FictionalHot, a new and challenging benchmark with recently curated questions requiring complex reasoning. Being intuitively reasonable and practically simple, extensive experiments show that agents trained with ReSeek significantly outperform SOTA baselines in task success rate and path faithfulness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feature Identification via the Empirical NTK"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:39:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:13:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T14:33:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-06T17:51:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-07T01:04:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We provide evidence that eigenanalysis of the empirical neural tangent kernel (eNTK) can surface feature directions in trained neural networks. Across three increasingly realistic settings -- a 1-layer MLP trained on modular addition, a 1-layer Transformer trained on modular addition and the pretrained language model Gemma-3-270M -- we show that top eigenspaces of the eNTK align with ground-truth or interpretable features. In the modular arithmetic examples, top eNTK eigenspaces align with the Fourier features used by the MLP and the Fourier features at seed-dependent frequencies used by the Transformer to implement known ground-truth algorithms. Moreover, the alignment of the relevant subspaces evolves over training, with its first derivative peaking near the onset of grokking. For Gemma-3-270M, we compute top eNTK eigendirections on a dataset of TinyStories context windows and check their alignment with an automatically-generated set of parts-of-speech and other grammatical feature directions. We find that the alignment of eNTK eigendirections with grammar features outperforms a same-budget baseline of PCA on model activations. These results suggest that eNTK eigenanalysis may provide a new handle towards identifying features in trained models for mechanistic interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 4 figures. v2: references and expanded discussion in Appendix B added. v3: Transformer case study and more appendices added. v4: Language model case study added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-07T02:18:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuxun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Fuxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Bianco","name":"Bianco, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Kovarskiy","name":"Kovarskiy, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Will","familyName":"LeVine","name":"LeVine, Will","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Dubbs","name":"Dubbs, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heming","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Heming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassandra","familyName":"Burgess","name":"Burgess, Cassandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suvam","familyName":"Bag","name":"Bag, Suvam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Patravali","name":"Patravali, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupanjali","familyName":"Kukal","name":"Kukal, Rupanjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikael","familyName":"Figueroa","name":"Figueroa, Mikael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Madhok","name":"Madhok, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Karianakis","name":"Karianakis, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjun","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jinjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unlocking Zero-Shot Geospatial Reasoning via Indirect Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T21:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T21:51:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:12:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training robust reasoning vision-language models (VLMs) in rare domains (such as geospatial) is fundamentally constrained by supervision scarcity. While raw geospatial imagery is abundant, the amount of task-direct supervision falls far behind that of common domains. In this work, we validate an important conclusion: indirect verifiable rewards, derived from seemingly unrelated metadata, are sufficient to induce sophisticated and generalizable geospatial reasoning across a wide range of downstream tasks (25+). We present Geo-R1 as one empirical instantiation of this paradigm. Rather than relying on limited task-specific annotations (i.e., direct rewards), Geo-R1 utilizes scalable, verifiable indirect proxy rewards based on cross-view alignment with metadata (geolocation information) to drive reinforcement learning at scale. Such indirect rewards successfully motivate the model to discover and internalize zero-shot geospatial reasoning across diverse tasks, achieving extraordinary zero-shot transfer on out-of-distribution benchmarks and even surpassing fully supervised specialists on certain benchmarks. These findings indicate that optimizing for indirect verifiable rewards may provide a scalable pathway to unlock generalized reasoning capabilities in rare domains with massive unlabeled data archives. Our code is availavle at: https://github.com/miniHuiHui/Geo-R1."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00626","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00626","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00626","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen-An","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tzu-Han","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tzu-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Silence Matters: The Impact of Irrelevant Audio on Text Reasoning in Large Audio-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T07:13:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:38:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-26T15:50:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large audio-language models (LALMs) unify speech and text processing, but their robustness in noisy real-world settings remains underexplored. We investigate how irrelevant audio, such as silence, synthetic noise, and environmental sounds, affects text reasoning tasks where audio is unnecessary. Across three text-based benchmarks, we find that even non-informative audio reduces accuracy and increases prediction volatility; the severity of interference scales with longer durations, higher amplitudes, and elevated decoding temperatures. Silence, often assumed neutral, destabilizes outputs as strongly as synthetic noise. While larger models show greater resilience, vulnerabilities persist across all evaluated systems. We further test mitigation strategies and find that prompting shows limited effectiveness, whereas self-consistency improves stability at the cost of increased computation. Our results reveal cross-modal interference as a key robustness challenge and highlight the need for efficient fusion strategies that preserve reasoning performance in the presence of irrelevant inputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00626","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:28Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:05:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Feldhus","name":"Feldhus, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laura","familyName":"Kopf","name":"Kopf, Laura","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpreting Language Models Through Concept Descriptions: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:51:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.blackboxnlp-1.8","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the decision-making processes of neural networks is a central goal of mechanistic interpretability. In the context of Large Language Models (LLMs), this involves uncovering the underlying mechanisms and identifying the roles of individual model components such as neurons and attention heads, as well as model abstractions such as the learned sparse features extracted by Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs). A rapidly growing line of work tackles this challenge by using powerful generator models to produce open-vocabulary, natural language concept descriptions for these components. In this paper, we provide the first survey of the emerging field of concept descriptions for model components and abstractions. We chart the key methods for generating these descriptions, the evolving landscape of automated and human metrics for evaluating them, and the datasets that underpin this research. Our synthesis reveals a growing demand for more rigorous, causal evaluation. By outlining the state of the art and identifying key challenges, this survey provides a roadmap for future research toward making models more transparent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at The Eight Workshop on Analyzing and Interpreting Neural Networks for NLP (BlackboxNLP), co-located with EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:18Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00861","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00861","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00861","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"An","name":"An, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhui","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faqiang","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Faqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cijun","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Cijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Erase to Improve: Erasable Reinforcement Learning for Search-Augmented LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:53:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T08:17:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:50:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While search-augmented large language models (LLMs) exhibit impressive capabilities, their reliability in complex multi-hop reasoning remains limited. This limitation arises from three fundamental challenges: decomposition errors, where tasks are incorrectly broken down; retrieval missing, where key evidence fails to be retrieved; and reasoning errors, where flawed logic propagates through the reasoning chain. A single failure in any of these stages can derail the final answer. We propose Erasable Reinforcement Learning (ERL), a novel framework that transforms fragile reasoning into a robust process. ERL explicitly identifies faulty steps, erases them, and regenerates reasoning in place, preventing defective logic from propagating through the reasoning chain. This targeted correction mechanism turns brittle reasoning into a more resilient process. Models trained with ERL, termed ESearch, achieve substantial improvements on HotpotQA, MuSiQue, 2Wiki, and Bamboogle, with the 3B model achieving +8.48% EM and +11.56% F1, and the 7B model achieving +5.38% EM and +7.22% F1 over previous state-of-the-art(SOTA) results. These findings suggest that erasable reinforcement learning provides a powerful paradigm shift for robust multi-step reasoning in LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00861","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:10:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:10:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Chongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Changsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Downgrade to Upgrade: Optimizer Simplification Enhances Robustness in LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T06:40:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T12:38:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:53:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T00:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-18T07:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:34:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) unlearning aims to surgically remove the influence of undesired data or knowledge from an existing model while preserving its utility on unrelated tasks. This paradigm has shown promise in addressing privacy and safety concerns. However, recent findings reveal that unlearning effects are often fragile: post-unlearning manipulations such as weight quantization or fine-tuning can quickly neutralize the intended forgetting. Prior efforts to improve robustness primarily reformulate unlearning objectives by explicitly assuming the role of vulnerability sources. In this work, we take a different perspective by investigating the role of the optimizer, independent of unlearning objectives and formulations, in shaping unlearning robustness. We show that the 'grade' of the optimizer, defined by the level of information it exploits, ranging from zeroth-order (gradient-free) to first-order (gradient-based) to second-order (Hessian-based), is tightly linked to the resilience of unlearning. Surprisingly, we find that downgrading the optimizer, such as using zeroth-order methods or compressed-gradient variants (e.g., gradient sign-based optimizers), often leads to stronger robustness. While these optimizers produce noisier and less precise updates, they encourage convergence to harder-to-disturb basins in the loss landscape, thereby resisting post-training perturbations. By connecting zeroth-order methods with randomized smoothing, we further highlight their natural advantage for robust unlearning. Motivated by these insights, we propose a hybrid optimizer that combines first-order and zeroth-order updates, preserving unlearning efficacy while enhancing robustness. Extensive experiments on the MUSE and WMDP benchmarks, across multiple LLM unlearning algorithms, validate that our approach achieves more resilient forgetting without sacrificing unlearning quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:13Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myungkyu","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Myungkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daewon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Daewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taeyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungmin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HAMLET: Switch your Vision-Language-Action Model into a History-Aware Policy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:15:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:41:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T06:41:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:28:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-15T17:01:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-16T01:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inherently, robotic manipulation tasks are history-dependent: leveraging past context could be beneficial. However, most existing Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have been designed without considering this aspect, i.e., they rely solely on the current observation, ignoring preceding context. In this paper, we propose HAMLET, a scalable framework to adapt VLAs to attend to the historical context during action prediction. Specifically, we introduce moment tokens that compactly encode perceptual information at each timestep. Their representations are initialized with time-contrastive learning, allowing them to better capture temporally distinctive aspects. Next, we employ a lightweight memory module that integrates the moment tokens across past timesteps into memory features, which are then leveraged for action prediction. Through empirical evaluation, we show that HAMLET successfully transforms a state-of-the-art VLA into a history-aware policy, especially demonstrating significant improvements on long-horizon tasks that require historical context. In particular, on top of GR00T N1.5, HAMLET achieves an average success rate of 76.4% on history-dependent real-world tasks, surpassing the baseline performance by 47.2%. Furthermore, HAMLET pushes prior art performance from 64.1% to 66.4% on RoboCasa Kitchen (100-demo setup) and from 95.6% to 97.7% on LIBERO, highlighting its effectiveness even under generic robot-manipulation benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Project page: https://myungkyukoo.github.io/hamlet/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunfeng","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yudi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yudi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mykola","familyName":"Pechenizkiy","name":"Pechenizkiy, Mykola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Foundation Models with Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Olympic-Level Physics Problem Solving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:57:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T09:55:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T02:32:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:20:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with foundation models has achieved strong performance across diverse tasks, but their capacity for expert-level reasoning-such as solving Olympiad-level physics problems-remains largely unexplored. Inspired by the way students prepare for competitions by reviewing past problems, we investigate the potential of RAG to enhance physics reasoning in foundation models. We introduce PhoPile, a high-quality multimodal dataset specifically designed for Olympiad-level physics, enabling systematic study of retrieval-based reasoning. PhoPile includes diagrams, graphs, and equations, capturing the inherently multimodal nature of physics problem solving. Using PhoPile, we benchmark RAG-augmented foundation models, covering both large language models (LLMs) and large multimodal models (LMMs) with multiple retrievers. Our results demonstrate that integrating retrieval with physics corpora can improve model performance, while also highlighting challenges that motivate further research in retrieval-augmented physics reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:05Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-15T02:24:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01152","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01152","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01152","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustafa Omer","familyName":"Gul","name":"Gul, Mustafa Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire","familyName":"Cardie","name":"Cardie, Claire","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanya","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Tanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MASH: Modeling Abstention via Selective Help-Seeking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:41:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:10:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-13T17:08:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T02:07:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs cannot reliably recognize their parametric knowledge boundaries and often hallucinate answers to outside-of-boundary questions. In this paper, we introduce MASH (Modeling Abstention via Selective Help-seeking), a training framework that readily extracts abstentions from LLMs. Our key idea is that any external help-seeking by an LLM, i.e. search tool use, can serve as a proxy for abstention if the external help (search) is appropriately penalized while also rewarding answer accuracy. MASH operationalizes this idea using reinforcement learning with a pay-per-search reward. We run experiments on three knowledge-intensive QA datasets. Our results show that MASH substantially improves upon the selective help-seeking performance of prior efficient search approaches; on multi-hop datasets, it improves answer accuracy by 7.6%. Furthermore, MASH demonstrates strong off-the-shelf abstention performance, showcasing behavior competitive with prior abstention methods that additionally require predetermining model knowledge boundaries to construct training data. Overall, we show MASH training effectively aligns search tool use with parametric knowledge, which can be successfully leveraged for making abstention decisions and efficient search tool use"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, with 15 dedicated to citations and appendix. 17 tables and 11 figures. Preprint, under review. Paper updated to reflect new title and results"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01152","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00635","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00635","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00635","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanxiang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Nanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxin","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enhan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Enhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daiheng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Daiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanxia","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yanxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeying","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yeying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Erased, But Not Forgotten: Erased Rectified Flow Transformers Still Remain Unsafe Under Concept Attack"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:12:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T10:33:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:31:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T08:16:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:31:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-01T08:20:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:36:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-08T03:02:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:21:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models have enabled impressive generative capabilities, but they also raise significant safety concerns due to the potential to produce harmful or undesirable content. While concept erasure has been explored as a mitigation strategy, most existing approaches and corresponding attack evaluations are tailored to Stable Diffusion (SD) and exhibit limited effectiveness when transferred to next-generation rectified flow transformers such as Flux. In this work, we present ReFlux, the first concept attack method specifically designed to assess the robustness of concept erasure in the latest rectified flow-based T2I framework. Our approach is motivated by the observation that existing concept erasure techniques, when applied to Flux, fundamentally rely on a phenomenon known as attention localization. Building on this insight, we propose a simple yet effective attack strategy that specifically targets this property. At its core, a reverse-attention optimization strategy is introduced to effectively reactivate suppressed signals while stabilizing attention. This is further reinforced by a velocity-guided dynamic that enhances the robustness of concept reactivation by steering the flow matching process, and a consistency-preserving objective that maintains the global layout and preserves unrelated content. Extensive experiments consistently demonstrate the effectiveness and efficiency of the proposed attack method, establishing a reliable benchmark for evaluating the robustness of concept erasure strategies in rectified flow transformers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00635","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:19:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Tiblias","name":"Tiblias, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irina","familyName":"Bigoulaeva","name":"Bigoulaeva, Irina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Balloccu","name":"Balloccu, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iryna","familyName":"Gurevych","name":"Gurevych, Iryna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hypothesis-Driven Feature Manifold Analysis in LLMs via Supervised Multi-Dimensional Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:30:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:02:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-07T13:36:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:56:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The linear representation hypothesis states that language models (LMs) encode concepts as directions in their latent space, forming organized, multidimensional manifolds. Prior work has largely focused on identifying specific geometries for individual features, limiting its ability to generalize. We introduce Supervised Multi-Dimensional Scaling (SMDS), a model-agnostic method for evaluating and comparing competing feature manifold hypotheses. We apply SMDS to temporal reasoning as a case study and find that different features instantiate distinct geometric structures, including circles, lines, and clusters. SMDS reveals several consistent characteristics of these structures: they reflect the semantic properties of the concepts they represent, remain stable across model families and sizes, actively support reasoning, and dynamically reshape in response to contextual changes. Together, our findings shed light on the functional role of feature manifolds, supporting a model of entity-based reasoning in which LMs encode and transform structured representations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in TMLR (March 2026) | OpenReview: https://openreview.net/forum?id=vCKZ40YYPr | Code: https://github.com/UKPLab/tmlr2026-manifold-analysis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:30:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Sholehrasa","name":"Sholehrasa, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Ghanaatian","name":"Ghanaatian, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doina","familyName":"Caragea","name":"Caragea, Doina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lisa A.","familyName":"Tell","name":"Tell, Lisa A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jim E.","familyName":"Riviere","name":"Riviere, Jim E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Jaberi-Douraki","name":"Jaberi-Douraki, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoPK: Leveraging LLMs and a Hybrid Similarity Metric for Advanced Retrieval of Pharmacokinetic Data from Complex Tables and Documents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T22:05:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-02T17:48:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-03T01:05:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pharmacokinetics (PK) plays a critical role in drug development and regulatory decision-making for human and veterinary medicine, directly affecting public health through drug safety and efficacy assessments. However, PK data are often embedded in complex, heterogeneous tables with variable structures and inconsistent terminologies, posing significant challenges for automated PK data retrieval and standardization. AutoPK, a novel two-stage framework for accurate and scalable extraction of PK data from complex scientific tables. In the first stage, AutoPK identifies and extracts PK parameter variants using large language models (LLMs), a hybrid similarity metric, and LLM-based validation. The second stage filters relevant rows, converts the table into a key-value text format, and uses an LLM to reconstruct a standardized table. Evaluated on a real-world dataset of 605 PK tables, including captions and footnotes, AutoPK shows significant improvements in precision and recall over direct LLM baselines. For instance, AutoPK with LLaMA 3.1-70B achieved an F1-score of 0.92 on half-life and 0.91 on clearance parameters, outperforming direct use of LLaMA 3.1-70B by margins of 0.10 and 0.21, respectively. Smaller models such as Gemma 3-27B and Phi 3-12B with AutoPK achieved 2-7 fold F1 gains over their direct use, with Gemma's hallucination rates reduced from 60-95% down to 8-14%. Notably, AutoPK enabled open-source models like Gemma 3-27B to outperform commercial systems such as GPT-4o Mini on several PK parameters. AutoPK enables scalable and high-confidence PK data extraction, making it well-suited for critical applications in veterinary pharmacology, drug safety monitoring, and public health decision-making, while addressing heterogeneous table structures and terminology and demonstrating generalizability across key PK parameters. Code and data: https://github.com/hosseinsholehrasa/AutoPK"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in IEEE ICTAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-03T02:14:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Eunki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Na Min","familyName":"An","name":"An, Na Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan Ju","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wan Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangryul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangryul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Thorne","name":"Thorne, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjung","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Hyunjung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Large Vision-Language Models Ready to Guide Blind and Low-Vision Individuals?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:55:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T10:51:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) demonstrate a promising direction for assisting individuals with blindness or low-vision (BLV). Yet, measuring their true utility in real-world scenarios is challenging because evaluating whether their descriptions are BLV-informative requires a fundamentally different approach from assessing standard scene descriptions. While the \"VLM-as-a-metric\" or \"LVLM-as-a-judge\" paradigm has emerged, existing evaluators still fall short of capturing the unique requirements of BLV-centric evaluation, lacking at least one of the following key properties: (1) High correlation with human judgments, (2) Long instruction understanding, (3) Score generation efficiency, and (4) Multi-dimensional assessment. To this end, we propose a unified framework to bridge the gap between automated evaluation and actual BLV needs. First, we conduct an in-depth user study with BLV participants to understand and quantify their navigational preferences, curating VL-GUIDEDATA, a large-scale BLV user-simulated preference dataset containing image-request-response-score pairs. We then leverage the dataset to develop an accessibility-aware evaluator, VL-GUIDE-S, which outperforms existing (L)VLM judges in both human alignment and inference efficiency. Notably, its effectiveness extends beyond a single domain, demonstrating strong performance across multiple fine-grained, BLV-critical dimensions. We hope our work lays as a foundation for automatic AI judges that advance safe, barrier-free navigation for BLV users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 14 figures, 28 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed A.","familyName":"Elhag","name":"Elhag, Ahmed A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Raja","name":"Raja, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Morehead","name":"Morehead, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel M.","familyName":"Blau","name":"Blau, Samuel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongtao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hongtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Tyrchan","name":"Tyrchan, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eva","familyName":"Nittinger","name":"Nittinger, Eva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett M.","familyName":"Morris","name":"Morris, Garrett M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael M.","familyName":"Bronstein","name":"Bronstein, Michael M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Inter-Atomic Potentials without Explicit Equivariance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.1; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T22:15:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T17:55:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T01:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T16:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-01T01:07:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate and scalable machine-learned inter-atomic potentials (MLIPs) are essential for molecular simulations ranging from drug discovery to new material design. Current state-of-the-art models enforce roto-translational symmetries through equivariant neural network architectures, a hard-wired inductive bias that can often lead to reduced flexibility, computational efficiency, and scalability. In this work, we introduce TransIP: Transformer-based Inter-Atomic Potentials, a novel training paradigm for interatomic potentials achieving symmetry compliance without explicit architectural constraints. Our approach guides a generic non-equivariant Transformer-based model to learn SO(3)-equivariance by optimizing its representations in the embedding space. Trained on the recent Open Molecules (OMol25) collection, a large and diverse molecular dataset built specifically for MLIPs and covering different types of molecules (including small organics, biomolecular fragments, and electrolyte-like species), TransIP attains comparable performance in machine-learning force fields versus state-of-the-art equivariant baselines. Further, compared to a data augmentation baseline, TransIP achieves 40% to 60% improvement in performance across varying OMol25 dataset sizes. More broadly, our work shows that learned equivariance can be a powerful and efficient alternative to equivariant or augmentation-based MLIP models. Our code is available at: https://github.com/Ahmed-A-A-Elhag/TransIP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 7 tables, 11 figures. Under review. Changes from v2 to v3: Added results for new experiments, training models for 80 epochs on OMol25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:38Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:30:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00316","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00316","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00316","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Rostami","name":"Rostami, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atik","familyName":"Faysal","name":"Faysal, Atik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reihaneh Gh.","familyName":"Roshan","name":"Roshan, Reihaneh Gh.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huaxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil","familyName":"Muralidhar","name":"Muralidhar, Nikhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Dong","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yu-Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Can Perform Automatic Modulation Classification via Discretized Self-supervised Candidate Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:20:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T17:33:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T01:05:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Identifying wireless modulation schemes is essential for cognitive radio, but standard supervised models often degrade under distribution shift, and training domain-specific wireless foundation models from scratch is computationally prohibitive. Large Language Models (LLMs) offer a promising training-free alternative via in-context learning, yet feeding raw floating-point signal statistics into LLMs overwhelms models with numerical noise and exhausts token budgets. We introduce DiSC-AMC, a framework that reformulates Automatic Modulation Classification (AMC) as an LLM reasoning task by combining aggressive feature discretization with nearest-neighbor retrieval over self-supervised embeddings. By mapping continuous features to coarse symbolic tokens, DiSC-AMC aligns abstract signal patterns with LLM reasoning capabilities and reduces prompt length by over $50$\\%. Simultaneously, utilizing a DINOv2 visual encoder to retrieve the $k_\\text{NN}$ most similar labeled exemplars provides highly relevant, query-specific context rather than generic class averages. On a 10-class benchmark, a fine-tuned 7B-parameter LLM using DiSC-AMC achieves $83.0$\\% in-distribution accuracy ($-10$\\,to\\,$+10$\\,dB) and $82.50$\\% out-of-distribution (OOD) accuracy ($-11$\\,to\\,$-15$\\,dB), outperforming supervised baselines.\n Comprehensive ablations on vanilla LLMs demonstrate the token efficiency of DiSC-AMC. A training-free $7$B LLM achieves $71$\\% accuracy using only $0.5$\\,K-token prompt,surpassing a $200$B-parameter baseline that relies on a $2.9$K-token prompt. Furthermore, similarity-based exemplar retrieval outperforms naive class-average selection by over $20$\\%. Finally, we identify a fundamental limitation of this pipeline. At extreme OOD noise levels ($-30$\\,dB), the underlying self-supervised representations collapse, degrading retrieval quality and reducing classification to random chance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00316","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:46Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-30T02:11:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00810","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00810","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00810","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny","familyName":"Kunz","name":"Kunz, Jenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iben Nyholm","familyName":"Debess","name":"Debess, Iben Nyholm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annika","familyName":"Simonsen","name":"Simonsen, Annika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Family Matters: Language Transfer and Merging for Adapting Small LLMs to Faroese"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:17:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:49:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-26T17:06:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T01:07:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate strategies for adapting small, efficient language models to Faroese, a low-resource North Germanic language. Starting from English-pretrained models, we apply continued pre-training on related Scandinavian languages -- individually or combined via model merging -- before fine-tuning on Faroese. We compare full fine-tuning with parameter-efficient adaptation via LoRA, assessing their effects on general language modeling performance, linguistic accuracy, and text comprehension. To address the lack of existing Faroese evaluation resources, we construct two new minimal-pair probing benchmarks, one for linguistic acceptability and one for text comprehension, and complement them with human evaluations conducted by native Faroese linguists. Our results show that transfer from related languages is essential, but the optimal source language is task-dependent: Icelandic improves linguistic accuracy, while Danish boosts reading comprehension. The choice of adaptation method likewise depends on the target task: LoRA yields stronger linguistic acceptability and marginally higher human evaluation scores, whereas full fine-tuning produces better comprehension performance and more robust downstream fine-tuning. Merging multiple related languages under full fine-tuning (but not LoRA) improves general language modeling, though its benefits in the linguistic acceptability and comprehension probes are less consistent."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00810","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Suhyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptLoop: Plug-and-Play Prompt Refinement via Latent Feedback for Diffusion Model Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:21:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T08:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent progress, reinforcement learning (RL)-based fine-tuning of diffusion models often struggles with generalization, composability, and robustness against reward hacking. Recent studies have explored prompt refinement as a modular alternative, but most adopt a feed-forward approach that applies a single refined prompt throughout the entire sampling trajectory, thereby failing to fully leverage the sequential nature of reinforcement learning. To address this, we introduce PromptLoop, a plug-and-play RL framework that incorporates latent feedback into step-wise prompt refinement. Rather than modifying diffusion model weights, a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is trained with RL to iteratively update prompts based on intermediate latent states of diffusion models. This design achieves a structural analogy to the Diffusion RL approach, while retaining the flexibility and generality of prompt-based alignment. Extensive experiments across diverse reward functions and diffusion backbones demonstrate that PromptLoop (i) achieves effective reward optimization, (ii) generalizes seamlessly to unseen models, (iii) composes orthogonally with existing alignment methods, and (iv) mitigates over-optimization and reward hacking while introducing only a practically negligible inference overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR26 poster. 25 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dadi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Dadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Qihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi R.","familyName":"Fung","name":"Fung, Yi R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Self-Evolving Benchmarks: Synthesizing Agent Trajectories via Test-Time Exploration under Validate-by-Reproduce Paradigm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:52:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:20:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T19:10:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:04:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T11:47:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:53:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and agent system designs have empowered agents with unprecedented levels of capability. However, existing agent benchmarks are showing a trend of rapid ceiling-hitting by newly developed agents, making it difficult to meet the demands for evaluating agent abilities. To address this problem, we propose the Trajectory-based Validated-by-Reproducing Agent-benchmark Complexity Evolution (TRACE) framework. This framework takes an original task from an existing benchmark and encourages agents to freely explore and evolve it into a new task with higher difficulty while recording validatable agent trajectories. The framework proceeds in three stages: (1) evolutionary proposal mining, which provides task evolution proposals through preliminary exploration and divergent thinking; (2) problem formation and free exploration, where proposals are conceptualized into feasible problem candidates and the agents then explore them freely while recording their execution trajectories; and (3) multi-level validation, which ensures that the evolved tasks are accompanied by validatable and reproducible trajectories. Experiments on the GAIA benchmark demonstrate that the TRACE framework consistently enhances task complexity while improving the reliability of correctness through validatable execution trajectories. In addition, our framework can successfully adapt to and improve reasoning datasets represented by AIME-2024. This work marks a paradigm shift from static, manually curated benchmarks to dynamic, self-evolving evaluation systems, providing a sustainable and challenging runway for agent development"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is a work in progress due to methodology refinement and further evaluation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:31:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrhman","familyName":"Werby","name":"Werby, Abdelrhman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Rotondi","name":"Rotondi, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Scaparro","name":"Scaparro, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai O.","familyName":"Arras","name":"Arras, Kai O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KeySG: Hierarchical Keyframe-Based 3D Scene Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:53:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T03:43:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T15:56:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T02:07:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, 3D scene graphs have emerged as a powerful world representation, offering both geometric accuracy and semantic richness. Combining 3D scene graphs with large language models enables robots to reason, plan, and navigate in complex human-centered environments. However, current approaches for constructing 3D scene graphs are semantically limited to a predefined set of relationships, and their serialization in large environments can easily exceed an LLM's context window. We introduce KeySG, a framework that represents 3D scenes as a hierarchical graph consisting of floors, rooms, objects, and functional elements, where nodes are augmented with multi-modal information extracted from keyframes selected to optimize geometric and visual coverage. The keyframes allow us to efficiently leverage VLMs to extract scene information, alleviating the need to explicitly model relationship edges between objects, enabling more general, task-agnostic reasoning and planning. Our approach can process complex and ambiguous queries while mitigating the scalability issues associated with large scene graphs by utilizing a hierarchical multi-modal retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline to extract relevant context from the graph. Evaluated across three distinct benchmarks, 3D object semantic segmentation, functional element segmentation, and complex query retrieval, KeySG outperforms prior approaches on most metrics, demonstrating its superior semantic richness and efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and video are available at https://keysg-lab.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongcheng","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yongcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zexu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bokai","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Bokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erxue","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Erxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Hengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaiqiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuaiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CurES: From Gradient Analysis to Efficient Curriculum Learning for Reasoning LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:41:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T06:08:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Curriculum learning plays a crucial role in enhancing the training efficiency of large language models (LLMs) on reasoning tasks. However, existing methods often fail to adequately account for variations in prompt difficulty or rely on simplistic filtering mechanisms to select prompt datasets within a narrow criterion range, resulting in significant computational waste. In this work, we approach the problem from the perspective of reinforcement learning gradient optimization, offering a systematic and theoretical investigation into how to improve the training efficiency of LLMs. We identify two key factors influencing training efficiency: the selection of training prompts and the allocation of rollout quantities across different prompts. Our theoretical analysis reveals that the sampling distribution of prompts dictates the convergence rate of gradient descent, while the allocation of the rollout quantity influences the consistency and stability of overall gradient updates. Based on these insights, we propose CurES, an efficient training method that accelerates convergence and employs Bayesian posterior estimation to minimize computational overhead. Experiments demonstrate that our CurES outperforms Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) by +3.30 points and +4.82 points with 1.5B and 7B models, respectively, and exceeds the best prior sample efficient methods by +2.12 points on average across eight math reasoning benchmarks. Additionally, CurES exhibits faster convergence compared to baselines, including GRPO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 10 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:02Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Aghaei","name":"Aghaei, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarthak","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Sarthak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth","familyName":"Arun","name":"Arun, Prashanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Sambamoorthy","name":"Sambamoorthy, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SecureBERT 2.0: Advanced Language Model for Cybersecurity Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:12:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T19:38:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:29:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/acsacw69556.2025.00071","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective analysis of cybersecurity and threat intelligence data demands language models that can interpret specialized terminology, complex document structures, and the interdependence of natural language and source code. Encoder-only transformer architectures provide efficient and robust representations that support critical tasks such as semantic search, technical entity extraction, and semantic analysis, which are key to automated threat detection, incident triage, and vulnerability assessment. However, general-purpose language models often lack the domain-specific adaptation required for high precision. We present SecureBERT 2.0, an enhanced encoder-only language model purpose-built for cybersecurity applications. Leveraging the ModernBERT architecture, SecureBERT 2.0 introduces improved long-context modeling and hierarchical encoding, enabling effective processing of extended and heterogeneous documents, including threat reports and source code artifacts. Pretrained on a domain-specific corpus more than thirteen times larger than its predecessor, comprising over 13 billion text tokens and 53 million code tokens from diverse real-world sources, SecureBERT 2.0 achieves state-of-the-art performance on multiple cybersecurity benchmarks. Experimental results demonstrate substantial improvements in semantic search for threat intelligence, semantic analysis, cybersecurity-specific named entity recognition, and automated vulnerability detection in code within the cybersecurity domain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:55Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimeesha","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Nimeesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimia","familyName":"Ghobadi","name":"Ghobadi, Kimia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Eliciting Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Time Series Analysis using Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:02:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-14T16:20:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T00:52:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Complex numerical time series analysis often demands multi-step reasoning capabilities beyond current models' reach. Tasks like medical diagnosis and weather forecasting require sequential reasoning processes - including counterfactual analysis, logical deduction, knowledge application, and multi-modal contextual integration - that existing time series models cannot explicitly perform. While recent research has shown large language models (LLMs) can achieve sophisticated Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning through reinforcement learning (RL), these advances have primarily focused on mathematical and coding domains, with LLMs still demonstrating poor performance on time series tasks. We introduce Chain Of thought for Understanding Numerical Time Series (COUNTS), the first framework that trains LLMs to perform CoT reasoning across diverse time series tasks using RL with verifiable rewards. Our approach employs a Residual Vector-Quantized VAE to create high-fidelity discrete tokens that seamlessly integrate into a pre-trained LLM's vocabulary. COUNTS undergoes a two-stage training process: first, supervised fine-tuning on time series analysis tasks to master our novel representations, followed by Group Relative Policy Optimization training on verifiable problems using prompting strategies that encourage explicit reasoning steps before producing final answers. Our experiments demonstrate that this RL-driven approach with intermediate CoT reasoning significantly enhances LLM performance across various time series analysis tasks, opening new possibilities for complex temporal data reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:01Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Parker","name":"Parker, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimeesha","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Nimeesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimia","familyName":"Ghobadi","name":"Ghobadi, Kimia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TsLLM: Augmenting LLMs for General Time Series Understanding and Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T16:54:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-14T16:24:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-17T00:52:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series data is fundamental to decision-making across many domains including healthcare, finance, power systems, and logistics. However, analyzing this data correctly often requires incorporating unstructured contextual information, answering domain-specific questions, and generating natural language explanations - capabilities that traditional time series models lack. While Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at contextual reasoning and knowledge integration, they struggle with numerical time series due to inefficient text-based representations and limited exposure to numerical data during pretraining. We address this gap by augmenting an LLM with specialized time series perception through a patch-based encoder-decoder architecture. We train this Time Series augmented LLM (TsLLM) on a large corpus of over 25 billion tokens of interleaved time series and text spanning diverse tasks: forecasting with contextual information, question-answering, anomaly detection, classification, report generation, and more, all unified as next token prediction. This training enables TsLLM to leverage both its language understanding and newly acquired temporal reasoning capabilities. While not designed to surpass specialized models on traditional benchmarks, TsLLM demonstrates strong performance on tasks requiring the integration of time series analysis with natural language - capabilities that existing approaches cannot provide. It also exhibits strong zero-shot and few-shot performance, showing it can adapt to new data without additional training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:16:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:16:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jinchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jiakai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Guoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Event Stream Slicing for Open-Vocabulary Event-Based Object Detection via Vision-Language Knowledge Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:03:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:40:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-11T02:15:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Event cameras offer advantages in object detection tasks due to high-speed response, low latency, and robustness to motion blur. However, event cameras lack texture and color information, making open-vocabulary detection particularly challenging. Current event-based detection methods are typically trained on predefined categories, limiting their ability to generalize to novel objects, where encountering previously unseen objects is common. Vision-language models (VLMs) have enabled open-vocabulary object detection in RGB images. However, the modality gap between images and event streams makes it ineffective to directly transfer CLIP to event data, as CLIP was not designed for event streams. To bridge this gap, we propose an event-image knowledge distillation framework that leverages CLIP's semantic understanding to achieve open-vocabulary object detection on event data. Instead of training CLIP directly on event streams, we use image frames as inputs to a teacher model, guiding the event-based student model to learn CLIP's rich visual representations. Through spatial attention-based distillation, the student network learns meaningful visual features directly from raw event inputs while inheriting CLIP's broad visual knowledge. Furthermore, to prevent information loss due to event data segmentation, we design a hybrid spiking neural network (SNN) and convolutional neural network (CNN) framework. Unlike fixed-group event segmentation methods, which often discard crucial temporal information, our SNN adaptively determines the optimal event segmentation moments, ensuring that key temporal features are extracted. The extracted event features are then processed by CNNs for object detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilge","familyName":"Acun","name":"Acun, Bilge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasoon","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Prasoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newsha","familyName":"Ardalani","name":"Ardalani, Newsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangmin","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Sangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alicia","familyName":"Golden","name":"Golden, Alicia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Yu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chien-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meghana","familyName":"Madhyastha","name":"Madhyastha, Meghana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neeraja J.","familyName":"Yadwadkar","name":"Yadwadkar, Neeraja J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carole-Jean","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Carole-Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Composer: A Search Framework for Hybrid Neural Architecture Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:16:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T18:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hybrid model architectures that combine computational primitives (e.g., Attention, MLP) in different ratios have shown promising performance beyond Transformers. Some studies have shown that different interleavings of primitives can affect model quality as well. However, prior works explore the hybrid model architecture design space manually. Due to the large design space and training costs, discovering hybrid models that combine key computational primitives for pre-training is challenging. In this work, we take a principled approach in designing a modular hybrid model architecture search framework -- Composer. Composer explores model architectures at a small scale and extrapolates the top-performing model architectures to a larger scale using our proposed scaling strategies. Using Composer, we discover new hybrid LLM architectures that outperform Llama 3.2. Compared to Llama 3.2 and previous state-of-the-art baselines, the new model architectures consistently reduce validation loss at parameter scales of 350M-3B and improve evaluation accuracy on the downstream tasks by up to 2.8-8.3% (1.1-3.1% on average) while improving both training and inference efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00307","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00307","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00307","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thierry","familyName":"Blankenstein","name":"Blankenstein, Thierry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassilis","familyName":"Plachouras","name":"Plachouras, Vassilis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunando","familyName":"Sengupta","name":"Sengupta, Sunando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yarin","familyName":"Gal","name":"Gal, Yarin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alasdair","familyName":"Paren","name":"Paren, Alasdair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adel","familyName":"Bibi","name":"Bibi, Adel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BiasBusters: Uncovering and Mitigating Tool Selection Bias in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T18:32:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:05:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents backed by large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on external tools drawn from marketplaces where multiple providers offer functionally equivalent options. This raises a critical fairness concern: systematic bias in tool selection can degrade user experience and distort competition by privileging certain providers over others. We introduce a benchmark of diverse tool categories, each containing multiple functionally equivalent tools, to systematically evaluate tool-selection bias. Using this benchmark, we evaluate seven LLMs and show that substantial bias persists, with models either fixating on a single provider or disproportionately favoring tools that appear earlier in the context. To uncover the sources of this behavior, we conduct controlled experiments that isolate the effects of tool features, exposed metadata (name, description, and parameters), and pre-training exposure. We find that (1) semantic alignment between user queries and tool metadata is the strongest driver of selection; (2) small perturbations to tool descriptions can significantly shift choices; and (3) repeated pre-training exposure to a single endpoint amplifies provider-level bias. Finally, we propose a lightweight mitigation strategy that first filters tools to a relevant subset and then samples uniformly, substantially reducing selection bias while maintaining strong task coverage. Our results highlight tool-selection bias as a key obstacle to the fair deployment of tool-augmented LLM agents. Our code and benchmark are publicly available at https://github.com/thierry123454/tool-selection-bias."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Camera Ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00307","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:33Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00726","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00726","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00726","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Minelli","name":"Minelli, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulio","familyName":"Turrisi","name":"Turrisi, Giulio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Barasuol","name":"Barasuol, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Semini","name":"Semini, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CroSTAta: Cross-State Transition Attention Transformer for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:44:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T14:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T02:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning robotic manipulation policies through supervised learning from demonstrations remains challenging when policies encounter execution variations not explicitly covered during training. While incorporating historical context through attention mechanisms can improve robustness, standard approaches process all past states in a sequence without explicitly modeling the temporal structure that demonstrations may include, such as failure and recovery patterns. We propose a Cross-State Transition Attention Transformer that employs a novel State Transition Attention (STA) mechanism to modulate standard attention weights based on learned state evolution patterns, enabling policies to better adapt their behavior based on execution history. Our approach combines this structured attention with temporal masking during training, where visual information is randomly removed from recent timesteps to encourage temporal reasoning from historical context. Evaluation in simulation shows that STA consistently outperforms standard attention approach and temporal modeling methods like TCN and LSTM networks, achieving more than 2x improvement over cross-attention on precision-critical tasks. The source code and data can be accessed at https://github.com/iit-DLSLab/croSTAta"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and data available at https://github.com/iit-DLSLab/croSTAta"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00726","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:07:22Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:07:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurbek","familyName":"Tastan","name":"Tastan, Nurbek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Horvath","name":"Horvath, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Nandakumar","name":"Nandakumar, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic Self-Organization in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:08:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:41:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:21:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent systems (MAS) based on Large Language Models (LLMs) have the potential to solve tasks that are beyond the reach of any single LLM. However, this potential can only be realized when the collaboration mechanism between agents is optimized. Specifically, optimizing the communication structure between agents is critical for fruitful collaboration. Most existing approaches rely on fixed topologies, pretrained graph generators, optimization over edges, or employ external LLM judges, thereby adding to the complexity. In this work, we introduce a response-conditioned framework that adapts communication on-the-fly. Agents independently generate responses to the user query and assess peer contributions using an approximation of the Shapley value. A directed acyclic graph (DAG) is then constructed to regulate the propagation of the responses among agents, which ensures stable and efficient message transmission from high-contributing agents to others. This graph is dynamically updated based on the agent responses from the previous collaboration round. Since the proposed framework enables the self-organization of agents without additional supervision or training, we refer to it as SelfOrg. The SelfOrg framework goes beyond task- and query-level optimization and takes into account the stochastic nature of agent responses. Experiments with both strong and weak LLM backends demonstrate robust performance, with significant gains in the weak regime where prior methods collapse. We also theoretically show that multiple agents increase the chance of correctness and that the correct responses naturally dominate the information flow."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:12Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchang","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Yongchang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Mou","name":"Mou, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TokMem: One-Token Procedural Memory for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:51:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:22:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-08T18:30:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:22:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are typically controlled via prompts, which must be repeatedly re-processed for every new query and are difficult to reuse modularly. We introduce TokMem, a procedural memory framework that compiles each reusable task procedure into a single trainable memory token. Each token serves as both a procedure index and a generation control signal that steers generation, enabling targeted behaviors with constant-size overhead. TokMem keeps the backbone LLM frozen and stores procedural knowledge entirely in these dedicated units, so new procedures can be added continually without interfering with existing ones. We evaluate TokMem on two settings: atomic recall over 1,000 Super-Natural Instructions tasks and compositional recall on multi-step function-calling. Our results show that TokMem consistently outperforms retrieval-augmented prompting while avoiding repeated context overhead. Moreover, it matches or exceeds parameter-efficient fine-tuning with substantially fewer trainable parameters."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yurun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Zeyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keting","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Keting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph2Eval: Automatic Multimodal Task Generation for Agents via Knowledge Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:37:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:11:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T05:47:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:32:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As multimodal LLM-driven agents advance in autonomy and generalization, traditional static datasets face inherent scalability limitations and are insufficient for fully assessing their capabilities in increasingly complex and diverse tasks. Existing studies have attempted to generate agent tasks using LLMs, but due to the inherent hallucinations of LLMs and the lack of internal data relationship modeling, these tasks often exhibit semantic inconsistencies and solvability issues. To address these challenges, we introduce Graph2Eval, a knowledge-graph-driven framework for automated, scalable, and semantically grounded agent task generation. At its core, Graph2Eval leverages a knowledge graph built from heterogeneous external data sources as a structured task space, generating multimodal agent tasks through subgraph sampling and task construction guided by task templates and meta-path strategies. To further ensure task reliability, a multi-stage filtering pipeline based on node reachability analysis, LLM scoring, and similarity analysis ensures the diversity and solvability of the generated tasks. By unifying both RAG Agent and Web Agent scenarios, Graph2Eval enables efficient generation of multimodal document understanding tasks and multi-step web interaction tasks. We instantiate the framework with Graph2Eval-Bench, a curated dataset of 1,319 tasks spanning document understanding and web interaction scenarios. Extensive experiments show that, on average, Graph2Eval improves task semantic consistency by 20% and solvability by 17% over baselines, while Graph2Eval-Bench effectively distinguishes agent performance, offering a new perspective on agent evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at CVPR 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-06T03:25:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyue Stella","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shuyue Stella","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avinandan","familyName":"Bose","name":"Bose, Avinandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faeze","familyName":"Brahman","name":"Brahman, Faeze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon Shaolei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Simon Shaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pang Wei","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Pang Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maryam","familyName":"Fazel","name":"Fazel, Maryam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulia","familyName":"Tsvetkov","name":"Tsvetkov, Yulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PrefDisco: Benchmarking Proactive Personalized Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:55:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-04T21:43:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:10:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current large language model (LLM) development treats task-solving and preference-alignment as separate challenges, optimizing first for objective correctness, then for alignment to aggregated human preferences. This paradigm fails in human-facing applications where solving a problem correctly is insufficient if the response mismatches the user's needs. This challenge intensifies in just-in-time scenarios where no prior user interaction history exists due to cold-start conditions or privacy constraints. LLMs need to proactively identify what they don't know about the user, strategically elicit preference values through questioning, then adapt their reasoning processes and responses accordingly -- a complicated chain of cognitive processes which we term personalized reasoning. We introduce PrefDisco, an evaluation methodology that transforms static benchmarks into interactive personalization tasks using psychologically-grounded personas with sparse, context-dependent preferences, and define PrefAlign as a fine-grained rubric-based metric for measuring preference alignment. PrefDisco builds scenarios where identical questions require different reasoning chains depending on user context, as optimal explanation approaches vary by individual expertise and preferences while maintaining factual accuracy. Evaluation of 21 frontier models across 10 tasks reveals 29.0% of naive personalization attempts produce worse preference alignment than generic responses, yet generic responses also fail to serve individual user needs. These findings suggest personalized reasoning requires dedicated development rather than emerging naturally. PrefDisco provides a foundation for developing systems that can adapt to individual users in education, healthcare, and technical domains where personalization is critical."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"65 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:21Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-06T03:25:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Simhi","name":"Simhi, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Herzig","name":"Herzig, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Tutek","name":"Tutek, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itay","familyName":"Itzhak","name":"Itzhak, Itay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idan","familyName":"Szpektor","name":"Szpektor, Idan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonatan","familyName":"Belinkov","name":"Belinkov, Yonatan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ManagerBench: Evaluating the Safety-Pragmatism Trade-off in Autonomous LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T10:31:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) evolve from conversational assistants into autonomous agents, evaluating the safety of their actions becomes critical. Prior safety benchmarks have primarily focused on preventing generation of harmful content, such as toxic text. However, they overlook the challenge of agents taking harmful actions when the most effective path to an operational goal conflicts with human safety. To address this gap, we introduce ManagerBench, a benchmark that evaluates LLM decision-making in realistic, human-validated managerial scenarios. Each scenario forces a choice between a pragmatic but harmful action that achieves an operational goal, and a safe action that leads to worse operational performance. A parallel control set, where potential harm is directed only at inanimate objects, measures a model's pragmatism and identifies its tendency to be overly safe. Our findings indicate that the frontier LLMs perform poorly when navigating this safety-pragmatism trade-off. Many consistently choose harmful options to advance their operational goals, while others avoid harm only to become overly safe and ineffective. Critically, we find this misalignment does not stem from an inability to perceive harm, as models' harm assessments align with human judgments, but from flawed prioritization. ManagerBench is a challenging benchmark for a core component of agentic behavior: making safe choices when operational goals and alignment values incentivize conflicting actions. Benchmark \u0026amp; code available at https://technion-cs-nlp.github.io/ManagerBench-website/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:10:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:10:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00438","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00438","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00438","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjun","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Dongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qishuai","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Qishuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyang","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Xiangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenfei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianzhu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zehuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BindWeave: Subject-Consistent Video Generation via Cross-Modal Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T07:58:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformer has shown remarkable abilities in generating high-fidelity videos, delivering visually coherent frames and rich details over extended durations. However, existing video generation models still fall short in subject-consistent video generation due to an inherent difficulty in parsing prompts that specify complex spatial relationships, temporal logic, and interactions among multiple subjects. To address this issue, we propose BindWeave, a unified framework that handles a broad range of subject-to-video scenarios from single-subject cases to complex multi-subject scenes with heterogeneous entities. To bind complex prompt semantics to concrete visual subjects, we introduce an MLLM-DiT framework in which a pretrained multimodal large language model performs deep cross-modal reasoning to ground entities and disentangle roles, attributes, and interactions, yielding subject-aware hidden states that condition the diffusion transformer for high-fidelity subject-consistent video generation. Experiments on the OpenS2V benchmark demonstrate that our method achieves superior performance across subject consistency, naturalness, and text relevance in generated videos, outperforming existing open-source and commercial models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00438","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anya","familyName":"Sims","name":"Sims, Anya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyu","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Keyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Changyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangxin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiangxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaopan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Shaopan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenmien","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenmien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuen Yang","familyName":"Beh","name":"Beh, Chuen Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weixun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weiyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Diyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Shieh","name":"Shieh, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yee Whye","familyName":"Teh","name":"Teh, Yee Whye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wee Sun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wee Sun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GEM: A Gym for Agentic LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:55:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T14:06:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:14:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The training paradigm for large language models (LLMs) is moving from static datasets to experience-based learning, where agents acquire skills via interacting with complex environments. To facilitate this transition we introduce GEM (General Experience Maker), an open-source environment simulator designed for the age of LLMs. Analogous to OpenAI-Gym for traditional reinforcement learning (RL), GEM provides a standardized framework for the environment-agent interface, including asynchronous vectorized execution for high throughput, and flexible wrappers for easy extensibility. GEM also features a diverse suite of environments, robust integrated tools, and single-file example scripts demonstrating using GEM with five popular RL training frameworks. Along with this, we also provide a set of baselines across 24 environments using REINFORCE with Return Batch Normalization (ReBN), which -- unlike GRPO -- is compatible with the full RL setting of dense per-turn rewards and offers better credit assignment. We further conduct apple-to-apple benchmarking of PPO, GRPO and REINFORCE in both single- and multi-turn settings using GEM to shed light on the algorithmic designs. Lastly, GEM also functions as a convenient evaluation toolkit besides a training environment. We hope this framework can help accelerate future agentic LLM research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:23Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00819","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00819","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00819","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luckeciano C.","familyName":"Melo","name":"Melo, Luckeciano C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Abate","name":"Abate, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yarin","familyName":"Gal","name":"Gal, Yarin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stabilizing Policy Gradients for Sample-Efficient Reinforcement Learning in LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:29:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:50:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T20:22:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:51:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement Learning, particularly through policy gradient methods, has played a central role in enabling reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models. However, the optimization stability of policy gradients in this setting remains understudied. As a result, existing implementations often resort to conservative hyperparameter choices to ensure stability, which requires more training samples and increases computational costs. Hence, developing models for reliably tracking the underlying optimization dynamics and leveraging them into training enables more sample-efficient regimes and further unleashes scalable post-training. We address this gap by formalizing the stochastic optimization problem of policy gradients with explicit consideration of second-order geometry. We propose a tractable computational framework that tracks and leverages curvature information during policy updates. We further employ this framework to design interventions in the optimization process through data selection. The resultant algorithm, Curvature-Aware Policy Optimization (CAPO), identifies samples that contribute to unstable updates and masks them out. Theoretically, we establish monotonic improvement guarantees under realistic assumptions. On standard math reasoning benchmarks, we empirically show that CAPO ensures stable updates under aggressive learning regimes where baselines catastrophically fail. With minimal intervention (rejecting fewer than 8% of tokens), CAPO achieves up to 30x improvement in sample efficiency over standard GRPO for LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00819","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Andong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kehai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kehai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefeng","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xuefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muyun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Muyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiejun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Tiejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Culture In a Frame: C$^3$B as a Comic-Based Benchmark for Multimodal Culturally Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T07:16:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T01:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:20:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cultural awareness capabilities have emerged as a critical capability for Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). However, current benchmarks lack progressed difficulty in their task design and are deficient in cross-lingual tasks. Moreover, current benchmarks often use real-world images. Each real-world image typically contains one culture, making these benchmarks relatively easy for MLLMs. Based on this, we propose C$^3$B (Comics Cross-Cultural Benchmark), a novel multicultural, multitask and multilingual cultural awareness capabilities benchmark. C$^3$B comprises over 2000 images and over 18000 QA pairs, constructed on three tasks with progressed difficulties, from basic visual recognition to higher-level cultural conflict understanding, and finally to cultural content generation. We conducted evaluations on 11 open-source MLLMs, revealing a significant performance gap between MLLMs and human performance. The gap demonstrates that C$^3$B poses substantial challenges for current MLLMs, encouraging future research to advance the cultural awareness capabilities of MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Roulet","name":"Roulet, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atish","familyName":"Agarwala","name":"Agarwala, Atish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Per-example gradients: a new frontier for understanding and improving optimizers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:00:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T00:10:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:29:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training algorithms in deep learning usually treat a mini-batch of samples as a single object; they average gradients over the mini-batch, and then process the average in various ways. Computing other statistics beyond the average may have been seen as prohibitively resource intensive in automatic differentiation (AD) frameworks. We show that this is not the case. Generally, gradient statistics can be implemented through a surgery of the AD graph, which, in some cases, incur almost no computational and memory overheads compared to the mini-batch gradient computation. Additionally, we show that in certain classes of models, including transformers, JAX's vectorization transformation offers a viable implementation for prototyping and experimentation. We then revise our understanding of two nonlinear operations in optimization through the lens of per-example gradient transformations. We first study signSGD and show that the optimal placement of the sign operation in the gradient processing chain is crucial to success and can be predicted with a simple signal-to-noise ratio argument. Next we study per-example variations of the Adam preconditioner, and show that optimization is best served when the preconditioner is dominated by the mean rather than the variance of the gradient distribution - in contrast to conventional wisdom. Overall we demonstrate that per-example gradient information enables new analyses and possibilities for algorithm design."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:49Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guancheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Less is More: Lean yet Powerful Vision-Language Model for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T05:14:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T15:30:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:46:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T09:00:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T01:34:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we reconceptualize autonomous driving as a generalized language problem and formulate the trajectory planning task as next waypoint prediction. We introduce Max-V1, a novel framework for one-stage end-to-end autonomous driving, named in tribute to the renowned Dutch racing driver Max Verstappen. Our framework presents a single-pass generation paradigm that aligns with the inherent sequentiality of driving. This approach leverages the generative capacity of the Vision-Language Model (VLM) to enable end-to-end trajectory prediction directly from front-view camera input. The efficacy of this method is underpinned by a principled supervision strategy derived from statistical modeling. This provides a well-defined learning objective, which makes the framework highly amenable to mastering complex driving policies through imitation learning from large-scale expert demonstrations. Empirically, our method achieves state-of-the-art performance on the nuScenes dataset, delivering an overall improvement of over 30% compared to prior baselines. Furthermore, it exhibits superior generalization performance on cross-domain datasets acquired from diverse vehicles, demonstrating notable potential for cross-vehicle robustness and adaptability. With these empirical strengths, this work introduces a model that enables fundamental driving behaviors, laying the foundation for the development of more capable self-driving agents. Code will be available upon publication."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:28Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-02T03:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00922","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00922","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00922","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shashank Reddy","familyName":"Chirra","name":"Chirra, Shashank Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayden","familyName":"Teoh","name":"Teoh, Jayden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praveen","familyName":"Paruchuri","name":"Paruchuri, Praveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pradeep","familyName":"Varakantham","name":"Varakantham, Pradeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Discovering Algorithms for Adversarial Imitation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:02:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:56:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T14:42:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:49:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adversarial Imitation Learning (AIL) methods, while effective in settings with limited expert demonstrations, are often considered unstable. These approaches typically decompose into two components: Density Ratio (DR) estimation $\\frac{ρ_E}{ρ_π}$, where a discriminator estimates the relative occupancy of state-action pairs under the policy versus the expert; and Reward Assignment (RA), where this ratio is transformed into a reward signal used to train the policy. While significant research has focused on improving density estimation, the role of reward assignment in influencing training dynamics and final policy performance has been largely overlooked. RA functions in AIL are typically derived from divergence minimization objectives, relying heavily on human design and ingenuity. In this work, we take a different approach: we investigate the discovery of data-driven RA functions, i.e, based directly on the performance of the resulting imitation policy. To this end, we leverage an LLM-guided evolutionary framework that efficiently explores the space of RA functions, yielding \\emph{Discovered Adversarial Imitation Learning} (DAIL), the first meta-learnt AIL algorithm. Remarkably, DAIL generalises across unseen environments and policy optimization algorithms, outperforming the current state-of-the-art of \\emph{human-designed} baselines. Finally, we analyse why DAIL leads to more stable training, offering novel insights into the role of RA functions in the stability of AIL. Code is publicly available: https://github.com/shshnkreddy/DAIL."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026 (Poster)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00922","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:10Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:15:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00981","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00981","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00981","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Leying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manthan","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Manthan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlexiCodec: A Dynamic Neural Audio Codec for Low Frame Rates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:56:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:59:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T02:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:20:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T08:24:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural audio codecs are foundational to speech language models. It is expected to have a low frame rate and decoupled semantic and acoustic information. A lower frame rate codec can reduce the computational cost of speech language models by shortening the sequence length. Recent studies have developed 12.5Hz low-frame-rate audio codecs, but even lower frame rate codecs remain underexplored. We find that a major challenge for very low frame rate tokens is missing semantic information. This paper introduces FlexiCodec to address this limitation. FlexiCodec improves semantic preservation with a dynamic frame rate approach and introduces a novel architecture featuring an ASR feature-assisted dual stream encoding and Transformer bottlenecks. With dynamic frame rates, it uses less frames at information-sparse regions through adaptively merging semantically similar frames. A dynamic frame rate also allows FlexiCodec to support inference-time controllable frame rates between 3Hz and 12.5Hz. Experiments on 6.25Hz, 8.3Hz and 12.5Hz average frame rates confirm that FlexiCodec excels over baseline systems in semantic information preservation and delivers a high audio reconstruction quality. We also validate the effectiveness of FlexiCodec in language model-based TTS. Demos are available at: https://flexicodec.github.io. Code is available at: https://github.com/amphionteam/flexicodec."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00981","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:38Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Hossein","familyName":"Samaei","name":"Samaei, Mohammad Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faryad Darabi","familyName":"Sahneh","name":"Sahneh, Faryad Darabi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lee W.","familyName":"Cohnstaedt","name":"Cohnstaedt, Lee W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caterina","familyName":"Scoglio","name":"Scoglio, Caterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EpidemIQs: Prompt-to-Paper LLM Agents for Epidemic Modeling and Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T18:54:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to accelerate complex interdisciplinary research domains. Epidemic modeling, characterized by its complexity and reliance on network science, dynamical systems, epidemiology, and stochastic simulations, represents a prime candidate for leveraging LLM-driven automation. We introduce EpidemIQs, a novel multi-agent LLM framework that integrates user inputs and autonomously conducts literature review, analytical derivation, network modeling, mechanistic modeling, stochastic simulations, data visualization and analysis, and finally documentation of findings in a structured manuscript, through five predefined research phases. We introduce two types of agents: a scientist agent for planning, coordination, reflection, and generation of final results, and a task-expert agent to focus exclusively on one specific duty serving as a tool to the scientist agent. The framework consistently generated complete reports in scientific article format. Specifically, using GPT 4.1 and GPT 4.1 Mini as backbone LLMs for scientist and task-expert agents, respectively, the autonomous process completes with average total token usage 870K at a cost of about $1.57 per study, successfully executing all phases and final report. We evaluate EpidemIQs across several different epidemic scenarios, measuring computational cost, workflow reliability, task success rate, and LLM-as-Judge and human expert reviews to estimate the overall quality and technical correctness of the generated results. Through our experiments, the framework consistently addresses evaluation scenarios with an average task success rate of 79%. We compare EpidemIQs to an iterative single-agent LLM, benefiting from the same system prompts and tools, iteratively planning, invoking tools, and revising outputs until task completion. The comparisons suggest a consistently higher performance of EpidemIQs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:34Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songqiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Songqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congying","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Congying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jialun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shing-Chi","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Shing-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Emulating Semantic Translation Help LLMs with Code Translation? A Study Based on Pseudocode"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:58:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T09:01:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-22T02:45:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3790101","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large language models (LLMs) show promising potential in code translation, they still struggle to generate accurate translations using the commonly adopted direct code-to-code translation approach, which converts an original program into the target programming language (PL) in a single step. Inspired by the success of incorporating intermediate steps to guide LLMs in resolving challenging tasks, in this study, we explore pseudocode-based code translation. This approach emulates human semantic translation by first interpreting the original program's intent and logic into pseudocode and then implementing it in the target PL. To understand the effectiveness of this underexplored approach, we present a systematic empirical study on pseudocode-based code translation, aiming to investigate its helpfulness in enhancing the direct translation approach, illuminate its effective usage, and identify its limitations. By comparing direct and pseudocode-based translation on 9,690 translation tasks across six PLs with five popular LLMs, we found that pseudocode-based translation can effectively complement direct translation, particularly when translating from flexible to rigid PLs and handling a low-training-resource PL. Based on the findings, we suggest combining the translation results of both approaches for test-based selection to leverage their complementary strengths. We also reveal the advantages of pseudocode-based translation in decoupling the code understanding and generation burden on complicated programs and mitigating distractions from PL-specific implementations in original programs, as well as its limitations due to incorrect, incomplete, or ambiguous pseudocode. Our study sheds light on the effective use of pseudocode-based translation and provides evidence to help enhance LLMs in code translation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology (TOSEM)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:07Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Benyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangzhong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guangzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guiquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junfeng","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Junfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Predictability of Reinforcement Learning Dynamics for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:31:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T15:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:53:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-22T03:43:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:41:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) are largely driven by reinforcement learning (RL), yet the underlying parameter dynamics during RL training remain poorly understood. This work identifies two fundamental properties of RL-induced parameter updates in LLMs: (1) Rank-1 Dominance, where the top singular subspace of the parameter update matrix nearly fully determines reasoning improvements, recovering over 99\\% of performance gains; and (2) Rank-1 Linear Dynamics, where this dominant subspace evolves linearly throughout training, enabling accurate prediction from early checkpoints. Extensive experiments across 8 LLMs and 7 algorithms validate the generalizability of these properties. More importantly, based on these findings, we propose AlphaRL, a plug-in acceleration framework that extrapolates the final parameter update using a short early training window, achieving up to 2.5 speedup while retaining \\textgreater 96\\% of reasoning performance without extra modules or hyperparameter tuning. This positions our finding as a versatile and practical tool for large-scale RL, opening a path toward principled, interpretable, and efficient training paradigm for LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"48 pages, 28 figures;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Yao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wei-Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Tateishi","name":"Tateishi, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shusuke","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Shusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Mitsufuji","name":"Mitsufuji, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VIRTUE: Visual-Interactive Text-Image Universal Embedder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:11:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:29:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-23T02:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:05:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal representation learning models have demonstrated successful operation across complex tasks, and the integration of vision-language models (VLMs) has further enabled embedding models with instruction-following capabilities. However, existing embedding models lack visual-interactive capabilities to specify regions of interest from users (e.g., point, bounding box, mask), which have been explored in generative models to broaden their human-interactive applicability. Equipping embedding models with visual interactions not only would unlock new applications with localized grounding of user intent, which remains unexplored, but also enable the models to learn entity-level information within images to complement their global representations for conventional embedding tasks. In this paper, we propose a novel Visual-InteRactive Text-Image Universal Embedder (VIRTUE) that extends the capabilities of the segmentation model and the vision-language model to the realm of representation learning. In VIRTUE, the segmentation model can process visual prompts that pinpoint specific regions within an image, thereby enabling the embedder to handle complex and ambiguous scenarios more precisely. To evaluate the visual-interaction ability of VIRTUE, we introduce a large-scale Segmentation-and-Scene Caption Retrieval (SCaR) benchmark comprising 1M samples that aims to retrieve the text caption by jointly considering the entity with a specific object and image scene. VIRTUE consistently achieves a state-of-the-art performance with significant improvements across 36 universal MMEB (3.1%-8.5%) and five visual-interactive SCaR (15.2%-20.3%) tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. 25 pages. Project page: https://sony.github.io/virtue/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:56Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:00:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego Ortiz","familyName":"Barbosa","name":"Barbosa, Diego Ortiz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Malegaonkar","name":"Malegaonkar, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Burbano","name":"Burbano, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Andersson","name":"Andersson, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"György","familyName":"Dán","name":"Dán, György","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrik","familyName":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Henrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvaro A.","familyName":"Cardenas","name":"Cardenas, Alvaro A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Drones that Think on their Feet: Sudden Landing Decisions with Embodied AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:39:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-19T05:56:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:25:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous drones must often respond to sudden events, such as alarms, faults, or unexpected changes in their environment, that require immediate and adaptive decision-making. Traditional approaches rely on safety engineers hand-coding large sets of recovery rules, but this strategy cannot anticipate the vast range of real-world contingencies and quickly becomes incomplete. Recent advances in embodied AI, powered by large visual language models, provide commonsense reasoning to assess context and generate appropriate actions in real time. We demonstrate this capability in a simulated urban benchmark in the Unreal Engine, where drones dynamically interpret their surroundings and decide on sudden maneuvers for safe landings. Our results show that embodied AI makes possible a new class of adaptive recovery and decision-making pipelines that were previously infeasible to design by hand, advancing resilience and safety in autonomous aerial systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:06Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-20T03:02:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harry","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Harry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Brandfonbrener","name":"Brandfonbrener, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eryk","familyName":"Helenowski","name":"Helenowski, Eryk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinal","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Mrinal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuejie","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Yuejie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik Abinav","familyName":"Sankararaman","name":"Sankararaman, Karthik Abinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized Parallel Scaling with Interdependent Generations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:33:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:09:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T23:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:09:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-16T19:52:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:05:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parallel LLM inference scaling involves sampling a set of $N\u0026gt;1$ responses for a single input prompt. However, these $N$ parallel responses tend to be generated independently from each other, partitioning compute resources and leaving potentially useful information in one generation untapped by others. This is in contrast to response length scaling where past computation is used in all future steps. For higher quality responses and response sets, we propose Bridge to generate interdependent responses in parallel by rethinking batched LLM hidden states as holistic tensors rather than independent slices. With only a small amount (2.8%-5.1%) of new parameters, Bridge improves the relative mean accuracy gains from reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards by up to 39% and boosts consistency of correct responses. Trained once, Bridge scales to any generation width, all with greater performance than independent generations, unlocking a more general mode of parallel scaling that effectively leverages information between sequences, compatible with any post-generation aggregation technique."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shojiro","familyName":"Yamabe","name":"Yamabe, Shojiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T05:41:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:21:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate tokens in parallel through iterative denoising, which can reduce latency and enable bidirectional conditioning. However, the safety risks posed by jailbreak attacks that exploit this inference mechanism are not well understood. In this paper, we reveal that DLMs have a critical vulnerability stemming from their iterative denoising process and propose a countermeasure. Specifically, our investigation shows that if an affirmative token for a harmful query appears at an intermediate step, subsequent denoising can be steered toward a harmful response even in aligned models. As a result, simply injecting such affirmative tokens can readily bypass the safety guardrails. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the vulnerability allows existing optimization-based jailbreak attacks to succeed on DLMs. Building on this analysis, we propose a novel safety alignment method tailored to DLMs that trains models to generate safe responses from contaminated intermediate states that contain affirmative tokens. Our experiments indicate that the proposed method significantly mitigates the vulnerability with minimal impact on task performance. Furthermore, our method improves robustness against conventional jailbreak attacks. Our work underscores the need for DLM-specific safety research. Our code is available at https://github.com/mdl-lab/dlm-priming-vulnerability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00634","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00634","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00634","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiayao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAKAN: Landmark-assisted Adaptive Kolmogorov-Arnold Network for Face Forgery Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T03:13:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:13:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-15T03:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:45:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of deepfake generation techniques necessitates robust face forgery detection algorithms. While methods based on Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Transformers are effective, there is still room for improvement in modeling the highly complex and non-linear nature of forgery artifacts. To address this issue, we propose a novel detection method based on the Kolmogorov-Arnold Network (KAN). By replacing fixed activation functions with learnable splines, our KAN-based approach is better suited to this challenge. Furthermore, to guide the network's focus towards critical facial areas, we introduce a Landmark-assisted Adaptive Kolmogorov-Arnold Network (LAKAN) module. This module uses facial landmarks as a structural prior to dynamically generate the internal parameters of the KAN, creating an instance-specific signal that steers a general-purpose image encoder towards the most informative facial regions with artifacts. This core innovation creates a powerful combination between geometric priors and the network's learning process. Extensive experiments on multiple public datasets show that our proposed method achieves superior performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 3 figures. This work has been accepted at ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00634","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:45:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunzhi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Churan","familyName":"Zhi","name":"Zhi, Churan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ruizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"McAuley","name":"McAuley, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexue","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zexue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BiasFreeBench: a Benchmark for Mitigating Bias in Large Language Model Responses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:56:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-15T19:30:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:05:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing studies on bias mitigation methods for large language models (LLMs) use diverse baselines and metrics to evaluate debiasing performance, leading to inconsistent comparisons among them. Moreover, their evaluations are mostly based on the comparison between LLMs' probabilities of biased and unbiased contexts, which ignores the gap between such evaluations and real-world use cases where users interact with LLMs by reading model responses and expect fair and safe outputs rather than LLMs' probabilities. To enable consistent evaluation across debiasing methods and bridge this gap, we introduce BiasFreeBench, an empirical benchmark that comprehensively compares eight mainstream bias mitigation techniques (covering four prompting-based and four training-based methods) on two test scenarios (multi-choice QA and open-ended multi-turn QA) by reorganizing existing datasets into a unified query-response setting. We further introduce a response-level metric, Bias-Free Score, to measure the extent to which LLM responses are fair, safe, and anti-stereotypical. Debiasing performances are systematically compared and analyzed across key dimensions: the prompting vs. training paradigm, model size, and generalization of different training strategies to unseen bias types. We release our benchmark, aiming to establish a unified testbed for bias mitigation research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:43Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:45:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun-ichiro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Shun-ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koki","familyName":"Morita","name":"Morita, Koki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Mukunoki","name":"Mukunoki, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hoshino","name":"Hoshino, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T04:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:24:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T14:08:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:52:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we propose VibeCodeHPC, a multi-agent system based on large language models (LLMs) for the automatic tuning of high-performance computing (HPC) programs on supercomputers. VibeCodeHPC adopts Claude Code as its backend and provides an integrated environment that facilitates program development in supercomputer settings. The system not only brings the Vibe Coding paradigm -- program development through natural language interaction with users -- to HPC programming, but also enables autonomous performance optimization with minimal user intervention through a sophisticated multi-agent design. To achieve these objectives, VibeCodeHPC implements three core functionalities: (1) configuration capabilities tailored to the unique development environments of supercomputers, (2) collaborative operation among multiple LLM agents with distinct roles -- Project Manager (PM), System Engineer (SE), Programmer (PG), and Continuous Deliverer (CD), and (3) long-term autonomous operation through agent activity monitoring and dynamic deployment mechanisms. This paper highlights one of the most powerful features of VibeCodeHPC: fully automated code optimization through autonomous operation without user intervention. Specifically, it demonstrates the performance optimization of CPU-based codes on GPU-equipped systems for matrix multiplication and a Poisson equation solver using Jacobi's iterative method. The results show that the multi-agent configuration employed in VibeCodeHPC enables faster and more reliable development of higher-performance code compared to a single-agent setup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianbin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Xianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenda","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Shenda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Copy-Paste to Mitigate Large Language Model Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-08T16:02:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:11:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enables large language models (LLMs) to generate contextually grounded responses, contextual faithfulness remains challenging as LLMs may not consistently trust provided context, leading to hallucinations that undermine reliability. We observe an inverse correlation between response copying degree and context-unfaithful hallucinations on RAGTruth, suggesting that higher copying degrees reduce hallucinations by fostering genuine contextual belief. We propose CopyPasteLLM, obtained through two-stage high-copying response preference training. We design three prompting methods to enhance copying degree, demonstrating that high-copying responses achieve superior contextual faithfulness and hallucination control. These approaches enable a fully automated pipeline that transforms generated responses into high-copying preference data for training CopyPasteLLM. On FaithEval, ConFiQA and PubMedQA, CopyPasteLLM achieves best performance in both counterfactual and original contexts, remarkably with 12.2% to 24.5% accuracy improvements on FaithEval over the best baseline, while requiring only 365 training samples -- 1/50th of baseline data. To elucidate CopyPasteLLM's effectiveness, we propose the Context-Parameter Copying Capturing algorithm. Interestingly, this reveals that CopyPasteLLM recalibrates reliance on internal parametric knowledge rather than external knowledge during generation. All codes are available at https://github.com/longyongchao/CopyPasteLLM"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:21:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Free Draft-and-Verification: Toward Lossless Parallel Decoding for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T23:45:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:44:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T03:37:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:28:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Large Language Models (DLLMs) have emerged as a new paradigm of language modeling beyond autoregressive next-token prediction. Taking advantage of their inherent modeling foundations, DLLMs have the great potential of efficient inference with parallel decoding algorithms, which enable multi-token prediction. However, the high generation quality often requires the number of decoding steps equal to the sequence length, which performs a one-token-per-step decoding, and existing parallel decoding algorithms, which yield suboptimal decoding paths, bring inference speedup at the cost of non-negligible performance degradation. To overcome this challenge, we introduce Free Draft-and-Verification (FreeDave), a novel fast decoding algorithm tailored for DLLMs that achieves lossless parallel decoding without any model modification or extra modules. Specifically, we propose an algorithm of parallel-decoded candidate generation and verification, which is theoretically guaranteed to use the fewest model forward calls to reproduce the same sequence generated by one-token-per-step decoding. By extensive evaluations on math reasoning and code generation benchmarks across different DLLMs, FreeDave is proven to accelerate the inference up to $2.83\\times$ without performance degradation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:14Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:41:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeon","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Suhyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye-eun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Ye-eun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew J.","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Andrew J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI-Based Stroke Rehabilitation Domiciliary Assessment System with ST_GCN Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T16:45:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-02T10:09:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:50:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective stroke recovery requires continuous rehabilitation integrated with daily living. To support this need, we propose a home-based rehabilitation exercise and feedback system. The system consists of (1) hardware setup with RGB-D camera and wearable sensors to capture stroke movements, (2) a mobile application for exercise guidance, and (3) an AI server for assessment and feedback. When a stroke user exercises following the application guidance, the system records skeleton sequences, which are then assessed by the deep learning model, RAST-G@ (Rehabilitation Assessment Spatio-Temporal Graph ATtention). The model employs a spatio-temporal graph convolutional network to extract skeletal features and integrates transformer-based temporal attention to figure out action quality. For system implementation, we constructed the NRC dataset, include 10 upper-limb activities of daily living (ADL) and 5 range-of-motion (ROM) collected from stroke and non-disabled participants, with Score annotations provided by licensed physiotherapists. Results on the KIMORE and NRC datasets show that RAST-G@ improves over baseline in terms of MAD, RMSE, and MAPE. Furthermore, the system provides user feedback that combines patient-centered assessment and monitoring. The results demonstrate that the proposed system offers a scalable approach for quantitative and consistent domiciliary rehabilitation assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages(except references), 7 figures 6 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:11Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:40:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houjun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Houjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikhar","familyName":"Murty","name":"Murty, Shikhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher D.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Christopher D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Róbert","familyName":"Csordás","name":"Csordás, Róbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thoughtbubbles: an Unsupervised Method for Parallel Thinking in Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:49:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T08:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current approaches for scaling inference-time compute in transformers train them to emit explicit chain-of-thought tokens before producing an answer. While these methods are powerful, they are limited because they cannot be applied during pretraining and rely solely on serially-generated, natural-language verbalization. In this work, we propose Thoughtbubbles, a transformer variant that natively performs parallel adaptive computation in latent space by learning to fork or delete residual streams. Thus, tokens requiring more computation can form a \"bubble\" of cloned residuals in the middle of the network. Crucially, this behavior is learned during pretraining with only language modeling loss. Using half of the training budget, Thoughtbubbles outperforms the perplexity and zero-shot evals of both standard decoder LMs and those using non-adaptive parallel computation approaches. These results hold across model sizes from 150M to 1.9B. Thoughtbubbles achieves competitive GSM8K results using half of the baseline's token budget. The implicit nature of our method enables models to begin learning adaptive computation at pretraining time, paving the way to unified train-time and test-time scaling behaviors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:24Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrhman","familyName":"Gaber","name":"Gaber, Abdelrhman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"ElMahdy","name":"ElMahdy, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssif","familyName":"Abuzied","name":"Abuzied, Youssif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Abd-Eltawab","name":"Abd-Eltawab, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamer","familyName":"ElBatt","name":"ElBatt, Tamer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedLLM-Align: Feature Extraction From Heterogeneous Clients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T14:06:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T03:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:35:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without sharing raw data, making it attractive for privacy-sensitive domains, e.g., healthcare, finance, and IoT. A major obstacle, however, is the potential heterogeneity of tabular data across clients, in practical settings, where schema mismatches and incompatible feature spaces prevent straightforward aggregation. To address this challenge, this paper proposes FedLLM-Align, a federated learning framework that leverages pretrained transformer based language models for feature extraction. Towards this objective, FedLLM-Align serializes tabular records into text and derives semantically aligned embeddings from a pretrained LLM encoder, e.g, DistilBERT, facilitating lightweight local classifier heads that can be trained in a federated manner using standard aggregation schemes, e.g., FedAvg, while keeping all raw data records local. To quantify the merits and trade-offs of FedLLM-Align, we evaluate the proposed framework on binary classification tasks from two different domains: i) Coronary heart disease prediction on partitioned Framingham Heart Study data, and ii) Customer churn prediction on a financial dataset. FedLLM-Align outperforms state-of-the-art baselines by up to 25% in terms of the F1 score, under simulated schema heterogeneity, and achieves a 65% reduction in the communication overhead. These results establish FedLLM-Align as a privacy-preserving and communication-efficient approach for federated training based on clients with heterogeneous tabular datasets, commonly encountered in practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Anugraha","name":"Anugraha, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shou-Yi","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Shou-Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Zilu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annie En-Shiun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Annie En-Shiun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derry Tanti","familyName":"Wijaya","name":"Wijaya, Derry Tanti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genta Indra","familyName":"Winata","name":"Winata, Genta Indra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"mR3: Multilingual Rubric-Agnostic Reward Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:36:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:10:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:15:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluation using Large Language Model (LLM) judges has been widely adopted in English and shown to be effective for automatic evaluation. However, their performance does not generalize well to non-English settings, and it remains unclear what constitutes effective multilingual training for such judges. In this paper, we introduce mR3, a massively multilingual, rubric-agnostic reward reasoning model trained on 72 languages, achieving the broadest language coverage in reward modeling to date. We present a comprehensive study of data and curriculum selection for training to identify effective strategies and data sources for building high-quality reward models, including support for reasoning in the target language. Our approach attains state-of-the-art performance on multilingual reward model benchmarks, surpassing much larger models (i.e., GPT-OSS-120B) while being up to 9x smaller, and its effectiveness is further confirmed through extensive ablation studies. Finally, we demonstrate the effectiveness of mR3 in off-policy preference optimization and validate the quality of its reasoning traces and rubric-based evaluations through human studies with 20 annotators across 12 languages, where mR3 models' reasoning is preferred, including for extremely low-resource languages that are entirely unseen during training. Our models, data, and code are available as open source at https://github.com/rubricreward/mr3."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:45Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel","familyName":"Levine","name":"Levine, Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Santerre","name":"Santerre, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander S.","familyName":"Young","name":"Young, Alexander S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. Barry","familyName":"Levine","name":"Levine, T. Barry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francis","familyName":"Campion","name":"Campion, Francis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Majid","familyName":"Sarrafzadeh","name":"Sarrafzadeh, Majid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PRISM-Consult: A Panel-of-Experts Architecture for Clinician-Aligned Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.1; I.2.4; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:00:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-19T06:17:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present PRISM-Consult, a clinician-aligned panel-of-experts architecture that extends the compact PRISM sequence model into a routed family of domain specialists. Episodes are tokenized as structured clinical events; a light-weight router reads the first few tokens and dispatches to specialist models (Cardiac-Vascular, Pulmonary, Gastro-Oesophageal, Musculoskeletal, Psychogenic). Each specialist inherits PRISM's small transformer backbone and token template, enabling parameter efficiency and interpretability. This initial study evaluates a scoped panel of five specialist families defined by high-impact ED diagnostic groups. On real-world Emergency Department cohorts, specialists exhibit smooth convergence with low development perplexities across domains, while the router achieves high routing quality and large compute savings versus consult-all under a safety-first policy. We detail the data methodology (initial vs.\\ conclusive ICD-9 families), routing thresholds and calibration, and report per-domain results to avoid dominance by common events. The framework provides a practical path to safe, auditable, and low-latency consult at scale, and we outline validation steps-external/temporal replication, asymmetric life-threat thresholds, and multi-label arbitration-to meet prospective clinical deployment standards."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:16:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:16:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:05:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jianghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Intrinsic Capabilities: A Paradigm for Data Curation in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T02:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T12:33:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (VLMs) achieve strong benchmark performance, but controlling their behavior through instruction tuning remains difficult. Reducing the budget of instruction tuning dataset often causes regressions, as heuristic strategies treat models as black boxes and overlook the latent capabilities that govern learning. We introduce Capability-Attributed Data Curation (CADC), a framework that shifts curation from task-specific heuristics to intrinsic capability analysis. CADC discovers intrinsic capabilities in an unsupervised manner from gradient-based learning trajectories, attributes training data to these capabilities via influence estimation, and curates capability-aware curricula through balanced selection and staged sequencing. This transforms black-box instruction tuning into a controllable, capability-driven process. With as little as 5% of the original data, CADC surpasses full-data training on multimodal benchmarks. These results validate intrinsic capabilities as the fundamental building blocks of model learning and establish CADC as a principle paradigm for instruction data curation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quy Minh","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Quy Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh Sao Khue","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Minh Sao Khue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khanh-Tung","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Khanh-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc-Hai","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duc-Hai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang-Quoc-Viet","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hoang-Quoc-Viet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang Thanh","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Hoang Thanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang D.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Hoang D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolBrain: A Flexible Reinforcement Learning Framework for Agentic Tools"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T16:01:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T04:21:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective tool use is essential for agentic AI, yet training agents to utilize tools remains challenging due to manually designed rewards, limited training data, and poor multi-tool selection, resulting in slow adaptation, wasted computational resources, and suboptimal performance. We introduce ToolBrain, a lightweight and user-friendly framework for training tool use in agentic models with flexible reinforcement learning, thereby easing the barriers for researchers and practitioners to adapt LLM-based agents to specific domains. It supports a wide range of training strategies, including reinforcement learning algorithms such as GRPO and DPO, as well as supervised learning. ToolBrain enables custom reward callables directly on an agent's execution traces or simply utilizes an automated LLM-as-a-judge system for reward generation. It is packed with useful capabilities, including knowledge distillation from large to small models, automatic task generation from tool descriptions, seamless tool retrieval, efficient fine-tuning pipelines with QLoRA through Unsloth, and quantized inference via bitsandbytes. We demonstrate ToolBrain through an Email Search Agent case study, showing measurable improvements in tool-use skills under a realistic workflow, while keeping the codebase simple and extensible. Our framework is publicly available at https://toolbrain.org/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:33Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00887","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00887","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00887","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyoung","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Soyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daeyong","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Daeyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishek","familyName":"Anand","name":"Anand, Avishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-won","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Seung-won","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Listwise Reranking for Corpus Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:34:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:54:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-21T15:31:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-23T01:51:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3773966.3779404","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reranker improves retrieval performance by capturing document interactions. At one extreme, graph-aware adaptive retrieval (GAR) represents an information-rich regime, requiring a pre-computed document similarity graph in reranking. However, as such graphs are often unavailable, or incur quadratic memory costs even when available, graph-free rerankers leverage large language model (LLM) calls to achieve competitive performance. We introduce L2G, a novel framework that implicitly induces document graphs from listwise reranker logs. By converting reranker signals into a graph structure, L2G enables scalable graph-based retrieval without the overhead of explicit graph computation. Results on the TREC-DL and BEIR subset show that L2G matches the effectiveness of oracle-based graph methods, while incurring zero additional LLM calls."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for WSDM 2026 (short)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00887","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:11:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:11:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-23T04:24:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eoin","familyName":"Quinn","name":"Quinn, Eoin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Carobene","name":"Carobene, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean","familyName":"Quentin","name":"Quentin, Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastien","familyName":"Boyer","name":"Boyer, Sebastien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Arbesú","name":"Arbesú, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Bent","name":"Bent, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoGraph: Geometric and Graph-based Ensemble Descriptors for Intrinsically Disordered Proteins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T11:13:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-18T11:13:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:39:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While deep learning has revolutionized the prediction of rigid protein structures, modelling the conformational ensembles of Intrinsically Disordered Proteins (IDPs) remains a key frontier. Current AI paradigms present a trade-off: Protein Language Models (PLMs) capture evolutionary statistics but lack explicit physical grounding, while generative models trained to model full ensembles are computationally expensive. In this work we critically assess these limits and propose a path forward. We introduce GeoGraph, a simulation-informed surrogate trained to predict ensemble-averaged statistics of residue-residue contact-map topology directly from sequence. By featurizing coarse-grained molecular dynamics simulations into residue- and sequence-level graph descriptors, we create a robust and information-rich learning target. Our evaluation demonstrates that this approach yields representations that are more predictive of key biophysical properties than existing methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at AI4Science and ML4PS NeurIPS Workshops 2025. v2: comparison with Human-IDRome model and link to github added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:32Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-19T03:17:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Heus","name":"Heus, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick","familyName":"Bookstaber","name":"Bookstaber, Rick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhruv","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Network-Knowledge Graph Duality: A Case Study in Agentic Supply Chain Risk Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Theoretical Economics (econ.TH)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:02:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T15:50:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:52:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) struggle with the complex, multi-modal, and network-native data underlying financial risk. Standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) oversimplifies relationships, while specialist models are costly and static. We address this gap with an LLM-centric agent framework for supply chain risk analysis. Our core contribution is to exploit the inherent duality between networks and knowledge graphs (KG). We treat the supply chain network as a KG, allowing us to use structural network science principles for retrieval. A graph traverser, guided by network centrality scores, efficiently extracts the most economically salient risk paths. An agentic architecture orchestrates this graph retrieval alongside data from numerical factor tables and news streams. Crucially, it employs novel ``context shells'' -- descriptive templates that embed raw figures in natural language -- to make quantitative data fully intelligible to the LLM. This lightweight approach enables the model to generate concise, explainable, and context-rich risk narratives in real-time without costly fine-tuning or a dedicated graph database."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 2nd Workshop on LLMs and Generative AI in Finance: International Conference on AI in Finance(ICAIF) 2025;7 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:16:59Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prithviraj","familyName":"Ammanabrolu","name":"Ammanabrolu, Prithviraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Practitioner's Guide to Multi-turn Agentic Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:23:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:09:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T07:53:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:22:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study what actually works and what doesn't for training large language models as agents via multi-turn reinforcement learning. Despite rapid progress, existing frameworks and definitions are fragmented, and there is no systematic formulation or analysis of which design choices matter across tasks. We address this gap by first breaking down the design space into three inter-related pillars -- environment, reward, and policy -- and empirically derive a recipe for training LLM agents in situated textual domains. In particular, we test TextWorld and ALFWorld, popular domains for testing situated embodied reasoning, as well as SWE-Gym for more software engineering style tasks. (i) For the environment, we analyze the impacts of task complexity in terms of sizes of the state and action spaces as well as optimal solution length, finding that even simple environments within a domain can provide signal on how well an agent can generalize to more complex tasks. (ii) For the reward, we ablate relative reward sparsity, observing that while dense turn-level rewards accelerate training, performance and stability is highly dependent on the choice of RL algorithm. (iii) And for the agent's policy, we explore the interplay between reward sparsity and biased (PPO, GRPO) and unbiased (RLOO) policy gradient methods in addition to showing how to find the optimal Supervised Fine-tuning (SFT) to RL training ratio given a fixed budget. We distill these findings into a training recipe that guides co-design across the three pillars, facilitating research and practical efforts in multi-turn agentic RL. Code: https://github.com/pearls-lab/meow-tea-taro"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:24Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:36:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Coda-Forno","name":"Coda-Forno, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuokai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhuokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dipesh","familyName":"Tamboli","name":"Tamboli, Dipesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsiao-Ping","familyName":"Tseng","name":"Tseng, Hsiao-Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring System 1 and 2 communication for latent reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:26:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:27:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-01T03:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:06:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Should LLM reasoning live in a separate module, or within a single model's forward pass and representational space? We study dual-architecture latent reasoning, where a fluent Base exchanges latent messages with a Coprocessor, and test two hypotheses aimed at improving latent communication over Liu et al. (2024): (H1) increase channel capacity; (H2) learn communication via joint finetuning. Under matched latent-token budgets on GPT-2 and Qwen-3, H2 is consistently strongest while H1 yields modest gains. A unified soft-embedding baseline, a single model with the same forward pass and shared representations, using the same latent-token budget, nearly matches H2 and surpasses H1, suggesting current dual designs mostly add compute rather than qualitatively improving reasoning. Across GSM8K, ProsQA, and a Countdown stress test with increasing branching factor, scaling the latent-token budget beyond small values fails to improve robustness. Latent analyses show overlapping subspaces with limited specialization, consistent with weak reasoning gains. We conclude dual-model latent reasoning remains promising in principle, but likely requires objectives and training schedules that explicitly shape latent spaces for algorithmic planning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:01:07Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:01:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:47:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00202","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00202","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00202","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingqi","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenrun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shenrun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarong","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Jiarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RouterArena: An Open Platform for Comprehensive Comparison of LLM Routers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:22:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-12T02:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:17:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T20:14:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's LLM ecosystem comprises a wide spectrum of models that differ in size, capability, and cost. No single model is optimal for all scenarios; hence, LLM routers have become essential for selecting the most appropriate model under varying circumstances. However, the rapid emergence of various routers makes choosing the right one increasingly challenging. To address this problem, we need a comprehensive router comparison and a standardized leaderboard, similar to those available for models. In this work, we introduce RouterArena, the first open platform enabling comprehensive comparison of LLM routers. RouterArena has (1) a principally constructed dataset with broad knowledge domain coverage, (2) distinguishable difficulty levels for each domain, (3) an extensive list of evaluation metrics, and (4) an automated framework for leaderboard updates. Leveraging our framework, we have produced the initial leaderboard with detailed metrics comparison as shown in Figure 1. Our framework for evaluating new routers is on https://github.com/RouteWorks/RouterArena. Our leaderboard is on https://routeworks.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 13 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00202","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-01T04:22:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":246,"totalPages":3,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-01T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-02T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-01T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-02T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29"}}